window.LUMEN_LOAD({"id": "probability", "lessons": {"axioms": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-we-need-a-language-for-uncertainty\" data-h=\"3\">Why we need a language for uncertainty</h3>\n<p>Will it rain tomorrow? Will this email turn out to be spam? Will a server get more than a thousand requests in the next second? None of these questions has a yes-or-no answer you can know in advance, yet we still make decisions about them every day. You take an umbrella, a spam filter moves a message, an engineer adds another machine. Probability is the part of mathematics that lets us talk about \"how likely\" in a precise, consistent way, so that different people (and different programs) reach the same conclusions from the same information.</p>\n<p>Machine learning is built on this language. A classifier that says \"this image is a cat with probability 0.92\" is making a probabilistic statement. A language model choosing the next word is sampling from a probability distribution. Before we can understand any of that, we need the basic vocabulary: experiments, outcomes, sample spaces and events. Then we need the three rules, called axioms, that every probability must obey.</p>\n<h3 id=\"experiments-and-outcomes\" data-h=\"3\">Experiments and outcomes</h3>\n<p>Imagine you are about to roll an ordinary six-sided die. Before you roll, you don't know what will come up, but you do know the full list of things that <em>could</em> come up: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or 6. In probability, any process with an uncertain result is called an <strong>experiment</strong>, and each possible result is an <strong>outcome</strong>. Rolling a die, flipping a coin, measuring tomorrow's temperature, and recording how long a web page takes to load are all experiments.</p>\n<p>The set of all possible outcomes is the <strong>sample space</strong>, written <span class=\"m\">S</span> or <span class=\"m\">\\Omega</span> (the Greek capital omega). For one die, <span class=\"m\">S = \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\}</span>. For one coin, <span class=\"m\">S = \\{H, T\\}</span>. For two coins flipped one after the other, the outcomes are pairs, so <span class=\"m\">S = \\{HH, HT, TH, TT\\}</span>. Notice that <span class=\"m\">HT</span> and <span class=\"m\">TH</span> are different outcomes: the first coin and the second coin are different objects. Writing the sample space out carefully is often the single most useful step in solving a probability problem.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Sample space</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The set <span class=\"m\">S</span> (or <span class=\"m\">\\Omega</span>) of all possible outcomes of an experiment. For a die, <span class=\"m\">S = \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\}</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"events-are-sets-of-outcomes\" data-h=\"3\">Events are sets of outcomes</h3>\n<p>Usually we care about a <em>group</em> of outcomes rather than one. \"Roll an even number\" happens if the die shows 2, 4 or 6. So we describe it as the set <span class=\"m\">E = \\{2, 4, 6\\}</span>. Any subset of the sample space is called an <strong>event</strong>. We say the event \"happens\" if the actual outcome lands inside that set. A single outcome like <span class=\"m\">\\{5\\}</span> is an event too, and so is the whole sample space <span class=\"m\">S</span> (something certainly happens) and the empty set <span class=\"m\">\\emptyset</span> (an impossible event, such as rolling a 7).</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Event</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Any subset of the sample space. \"Roll an even number\" is the event <span class=\"m\">\\{2, 4, 6\\}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<p>Because events are sets, we can combine them with set operations, and each operation has an everyday meaning. Let <span class=\"m\">A = \\{2, 4, 6\\}</span> (even) and <span class=\"m\">B = \\{4, 5, 6\\}</span> (at least 4).\nThe <strong>union</strong> <span class=\"m\">A \\cup B</span> means \"<span class=\"m\">A</span> or <span class=\"m\">B</span> (or both)\": <span class=\"m\">\\{2, 4, 5, 6\\}</span>.\nThe <strong>intersection</strong> <span class=\"m\">A \\cap B</span> means \"<span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span>\": <span class=\"m\">\\{4, 6\\}</span>.\nThe <strong>complement</strong> <span class=\"m\">A^c</span> means \"not <span class=\"m\">A</span>\": <span class=\"m\">\\{1, 3, 5\\}</span>.\nTwo events are <strong>disjoint</strong> (or mutually exclusive) if they share no outcomes, so they can never happen together. \"Roll a 1\" and \"roll an even number\" are disjoint.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Disjoint events</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Events <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> with <span class=\"m\">A \\cap B = \\emptyset</span>: they cannot both happen on the same trial.</p></div></div>\n\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"venn\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>Two events inside the sample space S. The overlap is A ∩ B; everything outside A is Aᶜ.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-three-axioms\" data-h=\"3\">The three axioms</h3>\n<p>A <strong>probability</strong> is a rule <span class=\"m\">P</span> that gives each event a number. In the 1930s the Russian mathematician Andrey Kolmogorov showed that you only need three rules, and everything else follows from them:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Non-negativity:</strong> <span class=\"m\">P(A) \\ge 0</span> for every event <span class=\"m\">A</span>.</li>\n<li><strong>Normalization:</strong> <span class=\"m\">P(S) = 1</span>. Something in the sample space certainly happens.</li>\n<li><strong>Additivity:</strong> if <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> are disjoint, then <span class=\"m\">P(A \\cup B) = P(A) + P(B)</span>. (The full version allows any countable list of disjoint events.)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>These rules match the way we think about proportions. If you picture probability as \"the fraction of a big pile of sand sitting on each region\", then no region can have negative sand, the whole pile is 1, and the sand on two separate regions adds up.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Probability axioms (Kolmogorov)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A probability <span class=\"m\">P</span> satisfies <span class=\"m\">P(A) \\ge 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(S) = 1</span>, and <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cup B) = P(A) + P(B)</span> whenever <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> are disjoint.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"what-follows-from-the-axioms\" data-h=\"3\">What follows from the axioms</h3>\n<p>From just three rules we can prove the facts you probably already use.</p>\n<p><strong>The complement rule.</strong> <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">A^c</span> are disjoint and together make up <span class=\"m\">S</span>. So <span class=\"m\">P(A) + P(A^c) = P(S) = 1</span>, which gives <span class=\"m\">P(A^c) = 1 - P(A)</span>. If the chance of rain is 0.3, the chance of no rain is 0.7.</p>\n<p><strong>The impossible event.</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\emptyset</span> is the complement of <span class=\"m\">S</span>, so <span class=\"m\">P(\\emptyset) = 1 - 1 = 0</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Probabilities are at most 1.</strong> Since <span class=\"m\">P(A^c) \\ge 0</span>, we get <span class=\"m\">P(A) = 1 - P(A^c) \\le 1</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>The addition rule for overlapping events.</strong> If <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> overlap, simply adding <span class=\"m\">P(A) + P(B)</span> counts the overlap twice. Subtracting it once fixes this:\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A\\cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A\\cap B)</span>\nWith the die, <span class=\"m\">P(A) = \\frac36</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(B) = \\frac36</span> and <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B) = P(\\{4,6\\}) = \\frac26</span>. So <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cup B) = \\frac36 + \\frac36 - \\frac26 = \\frac46</span>, which matches counting <span class=\"m\">\\{2, 4, 5, 6\\}</span> directly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"equally-likely-outcomes\" data-h=\"3\">Equally likely outcomes</h3>\n<p>When every outcome in a finite sample space is equally likely (a fair die, a well-shuffled deck), the axioms force each outcome to have probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{|S|}</span>, where <span class=\"m\">|S|</span> is the number of outcomes. Then any event's probability is just a count:\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A) = \\frac{|A|}{|S|} = \\frac{\\text{number of outcomes in } A}{\\text{total number of outcomes}}</span>\nFor two fair coins, <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{exactly one head}) = \\frac{|\\{HT, TH\\}|}{4} = \\frac24 = \\frac12</span>. This \"count and divide\" idea is so useful that the next lesson is devoted to counting.</p>\n<h3 id=\"where-do-the-numbers-come-from\" data-h=\"3\">Where do the numbers come from?</h3>\n<p>The axioms tell us how probabilities must <em>behave</em>, not what they <em>are</em>. In practice there are three common ways to assign them. The <strong>classical</strong> view uses symmetry: a fair die has six equally likely faces. The <strong>frequentist</strong> view says a probability is the long-run fraction of times an event happens if you repeat the experiment many times: if 30% of days with these weather conditions had rain, <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{rain}) = 0.3</span>. The <strong>Bayesian</strong> view treats probability as a degree of belief that gets updated with evidence. All three obey the same axioms, so all the rules in this course apply to all of them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>A frequent error is to treat outcomes as equally likely when they aren't. When you flip two coins, \"two heads\", \"two tails\" and \"one of each\" are <em>not</em> equally likely: \"one of each\" has two outcomes (<span class=\"m\">HT</span> and <span class=\"m\">TH</span>), so it has probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac12</span>, while each of the others has <span class=\"m\">\\frac14</span>. Listing the sample space with ordered outcomes avoids this trap.</p>\n<p>Another mistake is adding probabilities of events that overlap. <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{even or at least 4})</span> is not <span class=\"m\">\\frac36 + \\frac36 = 1</span>. Only add directly when the events are disjoint; otherwise subtract the overlap.</p>\n<p>A third mistake is forgetting the complement trick. \"At least one\" questions are usually much easier through \"none\". The chance of at least one six in four rolls is <span class=\"m\">1 - P(\\text{no sixes}) = 1 - \\left(\\frac56\\right)^4 \\approx 1 - 0.482 = 0.518</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><div class=\"box-label\">Tip</div><div class=\"box-title\">Use the complement</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Whenever you see \"at least one\", compute the probability of \"none\" and subtract it from 1.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>An experiment has a sample space <span class=\"m\">S</span> of outcomes; events are subsets of <span class=\"m\">S</span>. A probability assigns each event a number obeying three axioms: never negative, total 1, and additive for disjoint events. From these come the complement rule <span class=\"m\">P(A^c) = 1 - P(A)</span>, the addition rule <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A\\cap B)</span>, and, for equally likely outcomes, <span class=\"m\">P(A) = |A|/|S|</span>.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"venn\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Switch between union, intersection, conditional and complement, and change the three probabilities.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Choose the A ∪ B view and set <span class=\"m\">P(A) = 0.4</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(B) = 0.3</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B) = 0</span>. Read off the union.\n2. Now raise <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B)</span> to 0.12 and watch the union shrink to <span class=\"m\">0.4 + 0.3 - 0.12</span>.\n3. Switch to the Aᶜ view and check that the shaded area is always <span class=\"m\">1 - P(A)</span>.\n4. In the A ∩ B view, set <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B) = P(A)P(B)</span> (for example 0.4, 0.3 and 0.12) and read the message.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the overlap is exactly the part that would be counted twice if you added <span class=\"m\">P(A)</span> and <span class=\"m\">P(B)</span>. When the overlap is zero the events are disjoint and the plain addition axiom applies. The complement is everything outside <span class=\"m\">A</span>, which is why it always has probability <span class=\"m\">1 - P(A)</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">P(A)\\ge0,\\qquad P(S) = 1,\\qquad P(A\\cup B) = P(A)+P(B)\\ \\text{ if } A\\cap B=\\emptyset</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A^c) = 1 - P(A)\\qquad P(\\emptyset) = 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A\\cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A\\cap B)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A) = \\frac{|A|}{|S|}\\quad\\text{(equally likely outcomes)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A\\cup B\\cup C) = P(A)+P(B)+P(C) - P(A\\cap B) - P(A\\cap C) - P(B\\cap C) + P(A\\cap B\\cap C)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Addition rule (inclusion–exclusion for two events)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any events <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A\\cap B)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Split <span class=\"m\">A\\cup B</span> into two disjoint pieces: <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B\\cap A^c</span> (the part of <span class=\"m\">B</span> outside <span class=\"m\">A</span>). By additivity, <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cup B) = P(A) + P(B\\cap A^c)</span>. Also <span class=\"m\">B</span> is the disjoint union of <span class=\"m\">B\\cap A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B\\cap A^c</span>, so <span class=\"m\">P(B\\cap A^c) = P(B) - P(A\\cap B)</span>. Substituting gives the result.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Monotonicity</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">A\\subseteq B</span> then <span class=\"m\">P(A)\\le P(B)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">B</span> is the disjoint union of <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B\\cap A^c</span>, so <span class=\"m\">P(B) = P(A) + P(B\\cap A^c)\\ge P(A)</span> because the second term is non-negative.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Two fair dice are rolled. Find the probability that the sum is 7.</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>The sample space has <span class=\"m\">6\\times6 = 36</span> equally likely ordered pairs.</li>\n<li>The pairs with sum 7 are <span class=\"m\">(1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1)</span>: 6 outcomes.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(\\text{sum } 7) = \\frac{6}{36} = \\frac16</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">In a class, 60% study French, 50% study Spanish and 20% study both. What fraction study neither?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Let <span class=\"m\">F</span> and <span class=\"m\">E</span> be the two events. <span class=\"m\">P(F\\cup E) = 0.6 + 0.5 - 0.2 = 0.9</span>.</li>\n<li>\"Neither\" is the complement of \"at least one\": <span class=\"m\">1 - 0.9 = 0.1</span>.</li>\n<li>So 10% study neither language.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Probability of at least one head in 5 fair coin flips</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Complement: \"no heads\" means all five are tails.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(\\text{all tails}) = \\left(\\frac12\\right)^5 = \\frac{1}{32}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(\\text{at least one head}) = 1 - \\frac{1}{32} = \\frac{31}{32}\\approx 0.969</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A card is drawn from a standard 52-card deck. Let <span class=\"m\">A</span> = \"the card is a heart\" and <span class=\"m\">B</span> = \"the card is a face card (J, Q or K)\". Find <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cup B)</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">P(A) = \\frac{13}{52}</span> and <span class=\"m\">P(B) = \\frac{12}{52}</span>. The overlap is the three heart face cards, so <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B) = \\frac{3}{52}</span>. By the addition rule, <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cup B) = \\frac{13 + 12 - 3}{52} = \\frac{22}{52} = \\frac{11}{26}\\approx 0.423</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A classifier's output layer (softmax) produces a probability distribution over classes: every number is non-negative and they sum to 1, exactly the first two axioms. Treating the classes as disjoint events is what lets us add probabilities, for example <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{cat or dog}) = P(\\text{cat}) + P(\\text{dog})</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Randomized algorithms (hashing, quicksort with random pivots, load balancers) are analyzed with these rules. The <strong>union bound</strong> <span class=\"m\">P(A_1\\cup\\dots\\cup A_n)\\le\\sum_i P(A_i)</span>, which follows from the addition rule, is used constantly: if each of 1000 servers fails with probability <span class=\"m\">10^{-6}</span>, the chance that any fails is at most <span class=\"m\">10^{-3}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Weather forecasts are probabilities. \"70% chance of rain\" means that, on days with conditions like these, it rained about 70% of the time; the complement rule says there is a 30% chance of a dry day. Insurance companies use the same rules to price policies.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">P(A) = 0.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(B) = 0.4</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B) = 0.2</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cup B)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Addition rule: <span class=\"m\">0.5 + 0.4 - 0.2 = 0.7</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the probability of at least one head in 3 fair coin flips?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7/8|0.875\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">1 - P(\\text{TTT}) = 1 - \\frac18 = \\frac78</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Two fair coins are flipped. What is <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{exactly one head})</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/2|0.5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The outcomes <span class=\"m\">HT</span> and <span class=\"m\">TH</span> are 2 of the 4 equally likely outcomes.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">P(A) = 0.35</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">P(A^c)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.65\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Complement rule: <span class=\"m\">1 - 0.35</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Events <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> are disjoint with <span class=\"m\">P(A) = 0.3</span> and <span class=\"m\">P(B) = 0.5</span>. What is <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">0.15</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">0.8</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">0</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Disjoint events share no outcomes, so their intersection is empty and has probability 0.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which of these can NOT be a valid probability assignment for a coin, as <span class=\"m\">(P(H), P(T))</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">(0.5, 0.5)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">(0.9, 0.1)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">(0.7, 0.4)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The probabilities of all outcomes must add to 1 (normalization). <span class=\"m\">0.7 + 0.4 = 1.1</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "counting-probability": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-counting-matters-for-probability\" data-h=\"3\">Why counting matters for probability</h3>\n<p>In the last lesson we saw that when all outcomes are equally likely, a probability is a fraction: the number of outcomes in the event divided by the total number of outcomes. That sounds easy until the numbers get big. How many ways can a five-card poker hand be dealt? How many different passwords of length 8 are there? How likely is it that two people in a room of 23 share a birthday? Nobody lists millions of outcomes by hand. Instead we use a few counting rules that let us count huge sets quickly and correctly. This lesson builds those rules from scratch.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-multiplication-principle\" data-h=\"3\">The multiplication principle</h3>\n<p>Imagine you are getting dressed and have 3 shirts and 4 pairs of trousers. How many different outfits can you make? For each of the 3 shirts there are 4 trousers to pair with it, so there are <span class=\"m\">3\\times4 = 12</span> outfits. If you also choose one of 2 pairs of shoes, every one of those 12 outfits can go with either pair, so the total becomes <span class=\"m\">3\\times4\\times2 = 24</span>.</p>\n<p>This is the <strong>multiplication principle</strong>: if a task is done in a sequence of steps, and step 1 can be done in <span class=\"m\">n_1</span> ways, step 2 in <span class=\"m\">n_2</span> ways (no matter what happened in step 1), and so on, then the whole task can be done in <span class=\"m\">n_1\\times n_2\\times\\cdots\\times n_k</span> ways. Drawing a tree diagram makes this clear: each step splits every branch into the same number of new branches, and the number of leaves at the end is the product.</p>\n<p>A quick application: a 4-digit PIN uses digits 0 to 9, and digits may repeat. Each of the 4 positions has 10 choices, so there are <span class=\"m\">10^4 = 10{,}000</span> PINs. In general, choosing <span class=\"m\">k</span> things in order from <span class=\"m\">n</span> options <em>with replacement</em> (repeats allowed) gives <span class=\"m\">n^k</span> possibilities.</p>\n<h3 id=\"arranging-things-in-order-permutations\" data-h=\"3\">Arranging things in order: permutations</h3>\n<p>Now suppose repeats are not allowed. Five runners race, and we want to know how many different orders they can finish in. The winner can be any of 5 runners. Once the winner is fixed, second place can be any of the remaining 4, then third any of 3, then 2, then 1. By the multiplication principle there are <span class=\"m\">5\\times4\\times3\\times2\\times1 = 120</span> orders. This product is written <span class=\"m\">5!</span> and read \"5 factorial\". In general,\n<span class=\"mb\">n! = n\\times(n-1)\\times\\cdots\\times2\\times1,\\qquad 0! = 1.</span>\n(Defining <span class=\"m\">0! = 1</span> makes the formulas below work: there is exactly one way to arrange nothing.)</p>\n<p>Often we only care about the first few places. How many ways can gold, silver and bronze be awarded among 5 runners? That is <span class=\"m\">5\\times4\\times3 = 60</span>: we stop after three choices. An ordered selection of <span class=\"m\">k</span> items from <span class=\"m\">n</span> distinct items, without repeats, is called a <strong>permutation</strong>, and the count is\n<span class=\"mb\">P(n, k) = n\\times(n-1)\\times\\cdots\\times(n-k+1) = \\frac{n!}{(n-k)!}.</span>\nCheck: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{5!}{2!} = \\frac{120}{2} = 60</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Permutation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An ordered arrangement of <span class=\"m\">k</span> distinct items chosen from <span class=\"m\">n</span>. There are <span class=\"m\">P(n,k) = \\frac{n!}{(n-k)!}</span> of them.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"when-order-doesn-t-matter-combinations\" data-h=\"3\">When order doesn't matter: combinations</h3>\n<p>Many questions don't care about order. If you pick 3 friends out of 5 to come to the cinema, the group {Ana, Ben, Cy} is the same group whichever order you invite them in. Let's count carefully. There are <span class=\"m\">P(5,3) = 60</span> ordered lists of 3 friends. But every group of 3 people appears in that list <span class=\"m\">3! = 6</span> times (once for each way of ordering those three people). So the number of <em>groups</em> is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{60}{6} = 10</span>.</p>\n<p>This is the key idea: <strong>count with order, then divide by the number of orderings of each group</strong>. An unordered selection is called a <strong>combination</strong>, and the number of combinations of <span class=\"m\">k</span> items from <span class=\"m\">n</span> is\n<span class=\"mb\">\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!\\,(n-k)!},</span>\nread \"<span class=\"m\">n</span> choose <span class=\"m\">k</span>\". These numbers are also called <strong>binomial coefficients</strong> because they appear when you expand <span class=\"m\">(a + b)^n</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Combination</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An unordered selection of <span class=\"m\">k</span> items from <span class=\"m\">n</span>. There are <span class=\"m\">\\binom nk = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}</span> of them.</p></div></div>\n<p>A few values are worth knowing by heart: <span class=\"m\">\\binom n0 = \\binom nn = 1</span> (one way to choose nothing or everything), <span class=\"m\">\\binom n1 = n</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\binom n2 = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}</span>, the number of pairs. A group of 10 people can form <span class=\"m\">\\binom{10}{2} = 45</span> handshakes. Choosing which <span class=\"m\">k</span> to take is the same as choosing which <span class=\"m\">n - k</span> to leave, so <span class=\"m\">\\binom nk = \\binom{n}{n-k}</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"turning-counts-into-probabilities\" data-h=\"3\">Turning counts into probabilities</h3>\n<p>Now we can compute real probabilities. A lottery draws 6 numbers from 1 to 49, and order doesn't matter. The number of possible tickets is <span class=\"m\">\\binom{49}{6} = 13{,}983{,}816</span>, so a single ticket wins the jackpot with probability about 1 in 14 million.</p>\n<p>Here is a more typical example. A committee of 3 is chosen at random from 5 men and 4 women. What is the probability that exactly 2 women are chosen? The total number of committees is <span class=\"m\">\\binom93 = 84</span>. To get exactly 2 women we choose 2 of the 4 women, <span class=\"m\">\\binom42 = 6</span> ways, and 1 of the 5 men, <span class=\"m\">\\binom51 = 5</span> ways. By the multiplication principle that's <span class=\"m\">6\\times5 = 30</span> committees. So the probability is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{30}{84} = \\frac{5}{14}\\approx0.357</span>. This \"choose from each group, multiply, divide by the total\" pattern is called the <strong>hypergeometric</strong> pattern, and it appears whenever you sample without replacement from a population with two kinds of items.</p>\n<h3 id=\"arrangements-with-repeated-letters\" data-h=\"3\">Arrangements with repeated letters</h3>\n<p>How many different strings can you make by rearranging the letters of MISSISSIPPI? If all 11 letters were different there would be <span class=\"m\">11!</span> arrangements. But swapping the four S's among themselves gives the same string, and the same is true for the four I's and the two P's. So we divide out those repeats:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{11!}{4!\\,4!\\,2!} = 34{,}650.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"the-birthday-problem\" data-h=\"3\">The birthday problem</h3>\n<p>How many people need to be in a room before there's a better than even chance two share a birthday? Ignoring leap years and assuming all 365 days equally likely, use the complement: compute the probability that all birthdays are <em>different</em>. The first person can have any day, the second must avoid 1 day, the third must avoid 2, and so on. For <span class=\"m\">n</span> people,\n<span class=\"mb\">P(\\text{all different}) = \\frac{365\\times364\\times\\cdots\\times(365 - n + 1)}{365^n}.</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">n = 23</span> this is about <span class=\"m\">0.493</span>, so the chance of a shared birthday is about <span class=\"m\">0.507</span>. Most people guess a much larger number, because they think about matching <em>their own</em> birthday rather than counting all <span class=\"m\">\\binom{23}{2} = 253</span> pairs of people.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The most common error is using permutations when order doesn't matter, or the other way round. Ask yourself: \"If I swap two of the chosen items, is it a different outcome?\" If yes, use permutations; if no, use combinations. Another error is double counting with \"at least\" questions: counting \"at least one woman\" by first choosing one woman and then any others counts the same committee several times. Use the complement (\"no women\") instead. Finally, the counting formula <span class=\"m\">P(A) = |A|/|S|</span> only works if every outcome is equally likely, so make sure your sample space is built from equally likely outcomes, such as ordered dice rolls or equally likely hands.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The multiplication principle multiplies the number of choices at each step. Ordered selections without repeats are permutations, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{n!}{(n-k)!}</span>; unordered selections are combinations, <span class=\"m\">\\binom nk = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}</span>; with repeats allowed, ordered selections number <span class=\"m\">n^k</span>. Divide out orderings you don't care about. For equally likely outcomes, probability is a favourable count over a total count, and complements make \"at least one\" questions easy.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"counting\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> List every permutation or combination of a few letters.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Set <span class=\"m\">n = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">k = 2</span> with \"Order matters\" and count the listed strings (there should be 12).\n2. Switch to \"Order doesn't\" and notice that AB and BA collapse into one (6 remain).\n3. Increase <span class=\"m\">k</span> to 3 and compare: <span class=\"m\">P(4,3) = 24</span> against <span class=\"m\">\\binom43 = 4</span>. The ratio is <span class=\"m\">3! = 6</span>.\n4. Try <span class=\"m\">n = 6</span>, <span class=\"m\">k = 6</span> to see all <span class=\"m\">720</span> orderings start to fill the panel.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> every combination appears exactly <span class=\"m\">k!</span> times among the permutations, which is precisely why <span class=\"m\">\\binom nk = \\frac{P(n,k)}{k!}</span>. That's the \"count with order, then divide\" idea from the article.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"pascal\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Each number is the sum of the two above it; row n lists the values of n choose k.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Find row 5 (the sixth row, starting from row 0 at the top) and read <span class=\"m\">1, 5, 10, 10, 5, 1</span>.\n2. Check that <span class=\"m\">10 = 4 + 6</span>, the two entries above it.\n3. Add up a whole row and compare with <span class=\"m\">2^n</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\binom 5 2 = 10</span> is the number of 2-element committees from 5 people. Each row sums to <span class=\"m\">2^n</span> because <span class=\"m\">\\sum_k\\binom nk</span> counts all subsets of an <span class=\"m\">n</span>-element set, and each element is either in or out.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{ordered, with repeats: } n^k</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">n! = n(n-1)\\cdots 1,\\quad 0! = 1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(n,k) = \\frac{n!}{(n-k)!}\\qquad \\binom nk = \\frac{n!}{k!\\,(n-k)!}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\binom nk = \\binom n{n-k}\\qquad \\binom nk = \\binom{n-1}{k-1} + \\binom{n-1}{k}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{multiset arrangements: } \\frac{n!}{n_1!\\,n_2!\\cdots n_r!}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(a+b)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^n\\binom nk a^kb^{n-k}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Pascal's rule</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">1\\le k\\le n-1</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\binom nk = \\binom{n-1}{k-1} + \\binom{n-1}{k}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Fix one special item among the <span class=\"m\">n</span>. A <span class=\"m\">k</span>-element subset either contains it (then we choose the other <span class=\"m\">k-1</span> from the remaining <span class=\"m\">n-1</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\binom{n-1}{k-1}</span> ways) or doesn't (choose all <span class=\"m\">k</span> from the other <span class=\"m\">n-1</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\binom{n-1}{k}</span> ways). These cases are disjoint and cover everything, so the counts add.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Binomial theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">(a + b)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^n\\binom nk a^kb^{n-k}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Expanding the product of <span class=\"m\">n</span> factors <span class=\"m\">(a+b)</span> means picking <span class=\"m\">a</span> or <span class=\"m\">b</span> from each factor. The term <span class=\"m\">a^kb^{n-k}</span> appears once for every way to choose which <span class=\"m\">k</span> factors contribute an <span class=\"m\">a</span>, which is <span class=\"m\">\\binom nk</span> ways.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Two cards are dealt from a shuffled deck. What is the probability both are aces?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Unordered hands: <span class=\"m\">\\binom{52}{2} = \\frac{52\\times51}{2} = 1326</span>.</li>\n<li>Hands of two aces: <span class=\"m\">\\binom42 = 6</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P = \\frac{6}{1326} = \\frac{1}{221}\\approx0.0045</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">How many 5-card hands contain exactly 3 hearts?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Choose 3 of the 13 hearts: <span class=\"m\">\\binom{13}{3} = 286</span>.</li>\n<li>Choose the other 2 cards from the 39 non-hearts: <span class=\"m\">\\binom{39}{2} = 741</span>.</li>\n<li>Multiply: <span class=\"m\">286\\times741 = 211{,}926</span> hands.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">In how many ways can 8 people sit in a row if Ana and Ben must sit together?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Glue Ana and Ben into one block. Now arrange 7 units: <span class=\"m\">7! = 5040</span>.</li>\n<li>Inside the block they can sit as AB or BA: 2 ways.</li>\n<li>Total <span class=\"m\">2\\times5040 = 10{,}080</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A password is 4 characters long, each chosen uniformly from 26 lowercase letters. What is the probability that all 4 characters are different?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Total passwords: <span class=\"m\">26^4 = 456{,}976</span>. Passwords with all different letters: <span class=\"m\">26\\times25\\times24\\times23 = 358{,}800</span>. So <span class=\"m\">P = \\frac{358{,}800}{456{,}976}\\approx0.785</span>. Equivalently, <span class=\"m\">1\\cdot\\frac{25}{26}\\cdot\\frac{24}{26}\\cdot\\frac{23}{26}\\approx0.785</span>, the same reasoning as the birthday problem.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Counting is the heart of analyzing algorithms and security. A brute-force attack on an 8-character password over 62 symbols must try up to <span class=\"m\">62^8\\approx2.2\\times10^{14}</span> guesses. Hash collisions follow the birthday problem: with an <span class=\"m\">m</span>-bit hash, collisions become likely after about <span class=\"m\">2^{m/2}</span> items, which is why 64-bit hashes are too short for some uses.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Combinations count model and feature choices: picking 5 features out of 100 gives <span class=\"m\">\\binom{100}{5}\\approx7.5\\times10^7</span> subsets, which is why exhaustive feature selection is infeasible and greedy or regularized methods are used. The binomial coefficients also appear in the binomial distribution of the number of correct predictions.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Lotteries, card games and sports brackets are counting problems. A March-Madness style bracket of 63 games has <span class=\"m\">2^{63}\\approx9.2\\times10^{18}</span> possible fillings, so a \"perfect bracket\" is essentially impossible by guessing.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many ways can 4 books be arranged on a shelf?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"24\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">4! = 4\\times3\\times2\\times1 = 24</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is <span class=\"m\">\\binom{6}{2}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"15\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\frac{6\\times5}{2} = 15</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A club of 10 picks a president, a vice-president and a treasurer (different people). How many ways?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"720\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Order matters: <span class=\"m\">10\\times9\\times8 = 720</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A fair coin is flipped 4 times. What is the probability of exactly 2 heads?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3/8|0.375\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\binom42 = 6</span> of the <span class=\"m\">2^4 = 16</span> equally likely sequences.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Choosing a 5-card poker hand is a question of...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">permutations</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">combinations</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">choices with replacement</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The order in which you receive the cards doesn't change the hand.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many distinct arrangements are there of the letters in BOOK?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"12\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\frac{4!}{2!} = 12</span> because the two O's are identical.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "conditional": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"learning-something-changes-the-odds\" data-h=\"3\">Learning something changes the odds</h3>\n<p>Suppose a friend rolls a fair die behind a book and tells you only one thing: \"it's even.\" What is the chance it's a 6? Before the hint, <span class=\"m\">P(6) = \\frac16</span>. After the hint, the possible outcomes are just 2, 4 and 6, all still equally likely, so the chance is now <span class=\"m\">\\frac13</span>. The information didn't change the die; it changed what you <em>know</em>, and that shrank the set of outcomes you need to consider.</p>\n<p>This is <strong>conditional probability</strong>: the probability of an event <span class=\"m\">A</span> <em>given</em> that another event <span class=\"m\">B</span> has happened, written <span class=\"m\">P(A\\mid B)</span> and read \"probability of <span class=\"m\">A</span> given <span class=\"m\">B</span>.\" It is the most important idea in this course for machine learning, because almost every prediction is a conditional probability: the probability of a label given the features, the probability of the next word given the previous words, the probability of a disease given a test result.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition\" data-h=\"3\">The definition</h3>\n<p>When we learn that <span class=\"m\">B</span> happened, <span class=\"m\">B</span> becomes our new sample space. The only part of <span class=\"m\">A</span> that can still happen is the part inside <span class=\"m\">B</span>, which is <span class=\"m\">A\\cap B</span>. To make the probabilities inside the new universe add up to 1, we divide by <span class=\"m\">P(B)</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A\\mid B) = \\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)},\\qquad P(B) &gt; 0.</span>\nCheck with the die: <span class=\"m\">A = \\{6\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">B = \\{2, 4, 6\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">A\\cap B = \\{6\\}</span>. So <span class=\"m\">P(A\\mid B) = \\frac{1/6}{3/6} = \\frac13</span>, exactly what we reasoned out.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Conditional probability</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The probability of <span class=\"m\">A</span> given that <span class=\"m\">B</span> occurred: <span class=\"m\">P(A\\mid B) = \\dfrac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}</span>, defined when <span class=\"m\">P(B)&gt;0</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"reading-conditional-probabilities-from-a-table\" data-h=\"3\">Reading conditional probabilities from a table</h3>\n<p>Data often arrives as a table of counts, and conditional probabilities are just fractions of rows or columns. Imagine a company surveys 200 employees:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Satisfied</th>\n<th>Not satisfied</th>\n<th>Total</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Remote</td>\n<td>60</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Office</td>\n<td>60</td>\n<td>60</td>\n<td>120</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>120</td>\n<td>80</td>\n<td>200</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The overall chance an employee is satisfied is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{120}{200} = 0.6</span>. But given that someone works remotely, we only look at the Remote row: <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{satisfied}\\mid\\text{remote}) = \\frac{60}{80} = 0.75</span>. Given that someone is satisfied, we only look at the Satisfied column: <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{remote}\\mid\\text{satisfied}) = \\frac{60}{120} = 0.5</span>. These two numbers are different. In general <span class=\"m\">P(A\\mid B)\\ne P(B\\mid A)</span>, and mixing them up is one of the most common and most costly errors in reasoning with data.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-multiplication-rule-and-tree-diagrams\" data-h=\"3\">The multiplication rule and tree diagrams</h3>\n<p>Rearranging the definition gives the <strong>multiplication rule</strong>:\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A\\cap B) = P(A\\mid B)\\,P(B) = P(B\\mid A)\\,P(A).</span>\nThis is how we compute probabilities of things that happen in stages. Draw two cards without replacement. The chance the first is an ace is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{4}{52}</span>. Given that, 3 aces remain among 51 cards, so the chance the second is an ace is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{3}{51}</span>. Multiply: <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{both aces}) = \\frac{4}{52}\\cdot\\frac{3}{51} = \\frac{1}{221}</span>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>tree diagram</strong> makes this visual. Each branch is labelled with a conditional probability, and the probability of reaching a leaf is the product of the probabilities along its path. The rule extends to any number of stages: <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B\\cap C) = P(A)\\,P(B\\mid A)\\,P(C\\mid A\\cap B)</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-law-of-total-probability\" data-h=\"3\">The law of total probability</h3>\n<p>Sometimes <span class=\"m\">P(A)</span> is hard to find directly but easy once you split into cases. A factory has two machines. Machine 1 makes 60% of the parts and 2% of its parts are defective. Machine 2 makes 40% and 5% of its parts are defective. What fraction of all parts are defective? Split by machine:\n<span class=\"mb\">P(D) = P(D\\mid M_1)P(M_1) + P(D\\mid M_2)P(M_2) = 0.02\\times0.6 + 0.05\\times0.4 = 0.012 + 0.020 = 0.032.</span>\nIn general, if <span class=\"m\">B_1,\\dots,B_n</span> <strong>partition</strong> the sample space (they are disjoint and together cover everything), then <span class=\"m\">P(A) = \\sum_i P(A\\mid B_i)P(B_i)</span>. It's a weighted average of the conditional probabilities, with the case probabilities as weights. In a tree diagram, it means \"add up all the leaves where <span class=\"m\">A</span> happens.\"</p>\n<h3 id=\"independence-when-information-tells-you-nothing\" data-h=\"3\">Independence: when information tells you nothing</h3>\n<p>Sometimes learning <span class=\"m\">B</span> doesn't change the probability of <span class=\"m\">A</span> at all. Flip a coin and roll a die: knowing the coin landed heads tells you nothing about the die. We call <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> <strong>independent</strong> if\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A\\cap B) = P(A)\\,P(B),</span>\nwhich, when <span class=\"m\">P(B) &gt; 0</span>, is the same as <span class=\"m\">P(A\\mid B) = P(A)</span>.</p>\n<p>Independence isn't always obvious. Roll one die and let <span class=\"m\">A</span> = \"even\" <span class=\"m\">= \\{2,4,6\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">B = \\{1, 2\\}</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B) = P(\\{2\\}) = \\frac16</span>, and <span class=\"m\">P(A)P(B) = \\frac12\\cdot\\frac13 = \\frac16</span>. They are independent, even though they come from the same roll! Knowing the roll is 1 or 2 leaves the chance of \"even\" at <span class=\"m\">\\frac12</span>. Change <span class=\"m\">B</span> to <span class=\"m\">\\{1, 2, 3\\}</span> and the equality fails: <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B) = \\frac16</span> but <span class=\"m\">P(A)P(B) = \\frac14</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Independent events</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> are independent if <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B) = P(A)P(B)</span>; equivalently <span class=\"m\">P(A\\mid B) = P(A)</span>. Knowing one tells you nothing about the other.</p></div></div>\n<p>For more than two events, <strong>mutual independence</strong> requires the product rule for every sub-collection, not just every pair. Flip two fair coins and let <span class=\"m\">A</span> = \"first is heads\", <span class=\"m\">B</span> = \"second is heads\", <span class=\"m\">C</span> = \"both coins match\". Each pair is independent (check: <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap C) = P(HH) = \\frac14 = \\frac12\\cdot\\frac12</span>), but <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B\\cap C) = \\frac14\\ne\\frac18</span>. So the three are pairwise independent but not mutually independent.</p>\n<h3 id=\"conditional-independence\" data-h=\"3\">Conditional independence</h3>\n<p>Two events can be dependent overall but independent once you know a third fact. Shoe size and reading ability are correlated in children, because older children have bigger feet and read better. Given a child's age, shoe size tells you nothing more about reading. We write <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B\\mid C) = P(A\\mid C)P(B\\mid C)</span> and say <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> are <strong>conditionally independent given <span class=\"m\">C</span></strong>. This idea is the foundation of Naive Bayes classifiers and graphical models.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p><strong>Independent is not the same as disjoint.</strong> If two events with positive probability are disjoint, then learning that one happened tells you the other certainly did not: they are strongly <em>dependent</em>. <strong>Don't flip the condition.</strong> <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{positive test}\\mid\\text{disease})</span> can be 0.95 while <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{disease}\\mid\\text{positive test})</span> is under 0.1; the next lesson explains why. <strong>The gambler's fallacy:</strong> after five reds on a fair roulette wheel, black is not \"due.\" Independent spins have no memory.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Conditional probability <span class=\"m\">P(A\\mid B) = P(A\\cap B)/P(B)</span> restricts attention to the world where <span class=\"m\">B</span> happened. The multiplication rule <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B) = P(A\\mid B)P(B)</span> handles multi-stage experiments, and the law of total probability adds up cases. Events are independent when <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B) = P(A)P(B)</span>, meaning one gives no information about the other.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"venn\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Pick the A | B view to see conditioning as zooming in on B.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Choose the A | B view. Set <span class=\"m\">P(A) = 0.4</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(B) = 0.3</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B) = 0.12</span> and read <span class=\"m\">P(A\\mid B)</span>.\n2. Keep <span class=\"m\">P(B) = 0.3</span> and raise <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B)</span> to 0.24. Watch <span class=\"m\">P(A\\mid B)</span> climb to 0.8.\n3. Switch to A ∩ B and find the value of <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B)</span> that makes the panel say \"independent\".\n4. Set <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B) = 0</span> and look at <span class=\"m\">P(A\\mid B)</span> again.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> conditioning ignores everything outside <span class=\"m\">B</span> and asks what fraction of <span class=\"m\">B</span> is also in <span class=\"m\">A</span>. Independence is the special case where that fraction equals the fraction of the whole space that is in <span class=\"m\">A</span>. With zero overlap, <span class=\"m\">P(A\\mid B) = 0</span>: disjoint events are dependent.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">P(A\\mid B) = \\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A\\cap B) = P(A\\mid B)P(B) = P(B\\mid A)P(A)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A\\cap B\\cap C) = P(A)P(B\\mid A)P(C\\mid A\\cap B)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A) = \\sum_i P(A\\mid B_i)P(B_i)\\quad (B_i \\text{ a partition})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{independent: } P(A\\cap B) = P(A)P(B)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A^c\\mid B) = 1 - P(A\\mid B)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Law of total probability</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">B_1,\\dots,B_n</span> are disjoint events with positive probability whose union is <span class=\"m\">S</span>, then for any event <span class=\"m\">A</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(A) = \\sum_{i=1}^n P(A\\mid B_i)P(B_i)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The sets <span class=\"m\">A\\cap B_i</span> are disjoint and their union is <span class=\"m\">A</span>, so <span class=\"m\">P(A) = \\sum_i P(A\\cap B_i)</span> by additivity. By the multiplication rule each term is <span class=\"m\">P(A\\mid B_i)P(B_i)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Complements of independent events are independent</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> are independent, so are <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B^c</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B^c) = P(A) - P(A\\cap B) = P(A) - P(A)P(B) = P(A)(1 - P(B)) = P(A)P(B^c)</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A bag has 5 red and 3 blue marbles. Two are drawn without replacement. Find P(both red).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(\\text{first red}) = \\frac58</span>.</li>\n<li>Given that, 4 red remain among 7: <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{second red}\\mid\\text{first red}) = \\frac47</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(\\text{both red}) = \\frac58\\cdot\\frac47 = \\frac{20}{56} = \\frac{5}{14}\\approx0.357</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Using the factory above, a part is defective. Is it more likely from machine 1 or 2?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(D\\cap M_1) = 0.012</span> and <span class=\"m\">P(D\\cap M_2) = 0.020</span>, with <span class=\"m\">P(D) = 0.032</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(M_1\\mid D) = \\frac{0.012}{0.032} = 0.375</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(M_2\\mid D) = \\frac{0.020}{0.032} = 0.625</span>. Machine 2 is more likely, even though it makes fewer parts.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Three independent servers each fail with probability 0.1. Find P(at least one works).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>All three fail: <span class=\"m\">0.1^3 = 0.001</span> by independence.</li>\n<li>Complement: <span class=\"m\">1 - 0.001 = 0.999</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In the Monty Hall game, a car is behind one of three doors. You pick door 1. The host, who knows where the car is, always opens a different door with a goat behind it, then offers you the chance to switch. What is the probability of winning if you switch?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Your first pick is right with probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac13</span>; in that case switching loses. With probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac23</span> your first pick is a goat; then the host must open the only other goat door, so switching lands on the car. So <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{win by switching}) = \\frac23</span>. The host's choice gives information because it depends on where the car is.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>Naive Bayes</strong> classifiers assume features are conditionally independent given the class: <span class=\"m\">P(x_1,\\dots,x_n\\mid y) = \\prod_i P(x_i\\mid y)</span>. This is usually false, but it turns an impossible estimation problem into <span class=\"m\">n</span> easy ones, and it works surprisingly well for spam filtering and text classification. Language models use the multiplication rule (chain rule) to score a sentence: <span class=\"m\">P(w_1,\\dots,w_n) = \\prod_t P(w_t\\mid w_1,\\dots,w_{t-1})</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Reliability engineering uses independence: a system that needs all of <span class=\"m\">n</span> independent parts working succeeds with probability <span class=\"m\">\\prod_i p_i</span>, and a system with redundant copies fails only if every copy fails. Assuming independence when failures share a cause (same power supply, same bug) badly underestimates risk.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Medical screening, insurance and sports statistics are full of conditioning. \"The probability a 30-year-old smoker has a heart attack this year\" is a conditional probability given age and smoking status, and insurance prices are set from such tables.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Roll a fair die. What is <span class=\"m\">P(6\\mid\\text{even})</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Given \"even\", the outcomes are 2, 4, 6; one of them is a 6.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">P(A) = 0.3</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(B) = 0.5</span> and they're independent. Find <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.15\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Independence: <span class=\"m\">0.3\\times0.5</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B) = 0.2</span> and <span class=\"m\">P(B) = 0.5</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">P(A\\mid B)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.4|2/5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">0.2/0.5 = 0.4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Using the survey table, what is <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{satisfied}\\mid\\text{office})</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/2|0.5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>60 of the 120 office workers are satisfied.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Two events have positive probability and are disjoint. Are they independent?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes, always</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No, never</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">It depends</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>If one happens the other cannot, so <span class=\"m\">P(A\\cap B) = 0\\ne P(A)P(B)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Machines A and B make 70% and 30% of the parts, with defect rates 1% and 4%. What is P(defective)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.019\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">0.01\\times0.7 + 0.04\\times0.3 = 0.007 + 0.012 = 0.019</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "bayes": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-problem-of-reversing-a-condition\" data-h=\"3\">The problem of reversing a condition</h3>\n<p>Doctors, spam filters and detectives all face the same kind of question. We know how likely the <em>evidence</em> is if some <em>cause</em> is true, but we want the reverse: how likely the cause is, given the evidence we actually see. A medical test's sensitivity tells us <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{positive}\\mid\\text{disease})</span>. A patient wants <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{disease}\\mid\\text{positive})</span>. These two numbers can be wildly different, and Bayes' theorem is the exact rule that converts one into the other.</p>\n<p>Imagine a disease that affects 1% of people. A test catches 95% of people who have it, but it also gives a positive result to 10% of healthy people. You test positive. How worried should you be? Most people guess \"about 95%.\" The true answer is under 9%. By the end of this lesson you'll be able to compute it yourself and explain exactly why.</p>\n<h3 id=\"deriving-the-theorem\" data-h=\"3\">Deriving the theorem</h3>\n<p>The multiplication rule from the last lesson can be written two ways:\n<span class=\"mb\">P(H\\cap E) = P(E\\mid H)P(H) = P(H\\mid E)P(E).</span>\nHere <span class=\"m\">H</span> is a hypothesis (a possible cause, like \"has the disease\") and <span class=\"m\">E</span> is the evidence (\"tested positive\"). Divide both sides by <span class=\"m\">P(E)</span> and you get <strong>Bayes' theorem</strong>:\n<span class=\"mb\">P(H\\mid E) = \\frac{P(E\\mid H)\\,P(H)}{P(E)}.</span>\nThat's the whole derivation. The power lies in how the pieces are interpreted.</p>\n<h3 id=\"prior-likelihood-evidence-and-posterior\" data-h=\"3\">Prior, likelihood, evidence and posterior</h3>\n<p>Each term has a name.\nThe <strong>prior</strong> <span class=\"m\">P(H)</span> is how plausible the hypothesis was <em>before</em> seeing the evidence. For the disease, it's the base rate, 1%.\nThe <strong>likelihood</strong> <span class=\"m\">P(E\\mid H)</span> is how well the hypothesis predicts the evidence. If you have the disease, a positive result is very likely: 0.95.\nThe <strong>evidence</strong> (or marginal likelihood) <span class=\"m\">P(E)</span> is the overall probability of seeing this evidence, whatever the truth. We usually compute it with the law of total probability: <span class=\"m\">P(E) = P(E\\mid H)P(H) + P(E\\mid H^c)P(H^c)</span>.\nThe <strong>posterior</strong> <span class=\"m\">P(H\\mid E)</span> is the updated plausibility <em>after</em> the evidence.</p>\n<p>So Bayes' theorem says: posterior = likelihood × prior ÷ evidence. Or, in words: start with what you believed, and reweight it by how well each possibility explained what you saw.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Prior</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">P(H)</span>: the probability of a hypothesis before seeing the evidence.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Likelihood</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">P(E\\mid H)</span>: the probability of the observed evidence if the hypothesis is true.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Posterior</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">P(H\\mid E)</span>: the probability of the hypothesis after taking the evidence into account.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"working-the-disease-example\" data-h=\"3\">Working the disease example</h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"m\">D</span> be \"has the disease\" and <span class=\"m\">+</span> be \"tests positive.\" We know <span class=\"m\">P(D) = 0.01</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(+\\mid D) = 0.95</span> and <span class=\"m\">P(+\\mid D^c) = 0.10</span>.</p>\n<p>First, the top of the fraction: <span class=\"m\">P(+\\mid D)P(D) = 0.95\\times0.01 = 0.0095</span>.\nNext, the probability of a positive among healthy people: <span class=\"m\">P(+\\mid D^c)P(D^c) = 0.10\\times0.99 = 0.099</span>.\nAdd these to get the evidence: <span class=\"m\">P(+) = 0.0095 + 0.099 = 0.1085</span>.\nFinally, <span class=\"m\">P(D\\mid +) = \\frac{0.0095}{0.1085}\\approx0.088</span>.</p>\n<p>Only about 8.8% of people who test positive actually have the disease.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-the-answer-is-so-low-think-in-counts\" data-h=\"3\">Why the answer is so low: think in counts</h3>\n<p>Numbers like 0.0095 are hard to feel, so picture 10,000 people. About 100 of them have the disease; 95 of those test positive. Of the 9,900 healthy people, 10% (990) also test positive. So there are <span class=\"m\">95 + 990 = 1085</span> positive tests, and only 95 of them are real. <span class=\"m\">\\frac{95}{1085}\\approx8.8\\%</span>. The false positives outnumber the true positives ten to one, simply because there are so many more healthy people. This way of reasoning, called <strong>natural frequencies</strong>, is the easiest way to check any Bayes calculation.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key idea</div><div class=\"box-title\">Base rates matter</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Ignoring the prior is the <strong>base rate fallacy</strong>. When a condition is rare, even an accurate test produces mostly false alarms. A positive result raised the probability from 1% to 8.8%, nearly nine times higher, but not to 95%.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"updating-again-posteriors-become-priors\" data-h=\"3\">Updating again: posteriors become priors</h3>\n<p>Suppose the patient takes a second, independent test and it is also positive. Today's posterior becomes tomorrow's prior. Now <span class=\"m\">P(D) = 0.088</span> and\n<span class=\"mb\">P(D\\mid ++) = \\frac{0.95\\times0.088}{0.95\\times0.088 + 0.10\\times0.912} = \\frac{0.0836}{0.0836 + 0.0912}\\approx0.478.</span>\nAfter two positives it's close to a coin flip, and a third positive would push it to about 90%. This step-by-step updating is how Bayesian reasoning accumulates evidence.</p>\n<h3 id=\"odds-form\" data-h=\"3\">Odds form</h3>\n<p>There's a neat way to write Bayes' theorem with <strong>odds</strong>. The odds of <span class=\"m\">H</span> are <span class=\"m\">\\frac{P(H)}{P(H^c)}</span>. Then\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{P(H\\mid E)}{P(H^c\\mid E)} = \\frac{P(E\\mid H)}{P(E\\mid H^c)}\\times\\frac{P(H)}{P(H^c)}.</span>\nPosterior odds equal the <strong>likelihood ratio</strong> times the prior odds. For our test the likelihood ratio is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{0.95}{0.10} = 9.5</span>, and the prior odds are <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{99}</span>, so posterior odds are <span class=\"m\">\\frac{9.5}{99}\\approx0.096</span>, which is a probability of <span class=\"m\">\\frac{0.096}{1.096}\\approx0.088</span>. Same answer, and it shows clearly that each test multiplies the odds by 9.5.</p>\n<h3 id=\"many-hypotheses\" data-h=\"3\">Many hypotheses</h3>\n<p>Bayes' theorem works with any number of competing hypotheses <span class=\"m\">H_1,\\dots,H_n</span> that partition the possibilities:\n<span class=\"mb\">P(H_i\\mid E) = \\frac{P(E\\mid H_i)P(H_i)}{\\sum_j P(E\\mid H_j)P(H_j)}.</span>\nThe denominator is the same for every <span class=\"m\">H_i</span>, so to compare hypotheses you can just compare <span class=\"m\">P(E\\mid H_i)P(H_i)</span>. That's why people often write <span class=\"m\">P(H\\mid E)\\propto P(E\\mid H)P(H)</span>, \"posterior is proportional to likelihood times prior.\"</p>\n<h3 id=\"where-the-prior-comes-from\" data-h=\"3\">Where the prior comes from</h3>\n<p>A fair question is: where do we get the prior? Sometimes it is a measured base rate, like the share of the population with a disease or the share of past emails that were spam. Sometimes it comes from an earlier experiment, as in the two-test example above. And sometimes it is a reasonable judgement, such as \"before looking at the data, I think the coin is probably close to fair.\" Two people with different priors who see the same strong evidence will end up with posteriors that are close together, because the likelihood eventually swamps the prior. With little evidence, the prior matters a lot, and it is honest to say so and to check how sensitive the conclusion is to that choice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The biggest mistake is <strong>confusing <span class=\"m\">P(E\\mid H)</span> with <span class=\"m\">P(H\\mid E)</span></strong>, sometimes called the prosecutor's fallacy: \"the chance of this DNA match if the suspect is innocent is one in a million, so the chance he is innocent is one in a million.\" Wrong: in a city of 10 million, about 10 innocent people would match too. Another mistake is forgetting the false-positive branch when computing <span class=\"m\">P(E)</span>. A third is treating the prior as optional. With very strong evidence the prior matters less, but with weak evidence or rare events it dominates.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Bayes' theorem, <span class=\"m\">P(H\\mid E) = \\frac{P(E\\mid H)P(H)}{P(E)}</span>, turns \"how likely is the evidence if the cause is true\" into \"how likely is the cause given the evidence.\" The posterior combines the prior and the likelihood; the evidence term is found with total probability. Thinking in counts of people makes the calculation intuitive, and rare conditions produce many false positives.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"bayes\" data-args='{&quot;prior&quot;: 0.01}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> A 95%-sensitive test for a rare disease. Most positives are false positives. Change the prior and watch the answer move.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Start with prior 0.01, sensitivity 0.95, specificity 0.90. Confirm the posterior is about 0.088.\n2. Raise specificity to 0.99 (only 1% false positives). The posterior jumps to about 0.49.\n3. Put specificity back to 0.90 and raise the prior to 0.2, as if testing only people with symptoms.\n4. Push sensitivity all the way to 1.0 and notice how little that helps when the prior is 0.01.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the red sliver (true positives) and the orange block (false positives) together make up all positives. The posterior is the red sliver's share. When the disease is rare, the orange block is large even for a good test, so reducing false positives (specificity) or raising the prior matters much more than increasing sensitivity.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">P(H\\mid E) = \\frac{P(E\\mid H)P(H)}{P(E)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(E) = P(E\\mid H)P(H) + P(E\\mid H^c)P(H^c)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(H_i\\mid E) = \\frac{P(E\\mid H_i)P(H_i)}{\\sum_j P(E\\mid H_j)P(H_j)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{posterior odds} = \\frac{P(E\\mid H)}{P(E\\mid H^c)}\\times\\text{prior odds}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">p(\\theta\\mid\\text{data})\\propto p(\\text{data}\\mid\\theta)\\,p(\\theta)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Bayes' theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For events <span class=\"m\">H</span> and <span class=\"m\">E</span> with <span class=\"m\">P(E)&gt;0</span>: <span class=\"m\">P(H\\mid E) = \\dfrac{P(E\\mid H)P(H)}{P(E)}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>By the definition of conditional probability, <span class=\"m\">P(H\\cap E) = P(H\\mid E)P(E)</span> and also <span class=\"m\">P(H\\cap E) = P(E\\mid H)P(H)</span>. Set these equal and divide by <span class=\"m\">P(E)</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">1% have a disease; the test catches 95% of cases and has a 10% false-positive rate. Find P(disease | positive).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(+\\mid D)P(D) = 0.95\\times0.01 = 0.0095</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(+\\mid D^c)P(D^c) = 0.10\\times0.99 = 0.099</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(+) = 0.0095 + 0.099 = 0.1085</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(D\\mid +) = 0.0095/0.1085\\approx0.088</span>, about 8.8%.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">30% of emails are spam. The word \"free\" appears in 40% of spam and 5% of normal emails. An email contains \"free\". P(spam)?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(\\text{free}\\mid S)P(S) = 0.4\\times0.3 = 0.12</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(\\text{free}\\mid S^c)P(S^c) = 0.05\\times0.7 = 0.035</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(S\\mid\\text{free}) = \\frac{0.12}{0.12 + 0.035} = \\frac{0.12}{0.155}\\approx0.774</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Box 1 has 3 red and 1 blue ball; box 2 has 1 red and 3 blue. A fair coin picks a box and a ball is drawn. It is red. P(box 1)?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(R\\mid B_1)P(B_1) = \\frac34\\cdot\\frac12 = \\frac38</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(R\\mid B_2)P(B_2) = \\frac14\\cdot\\frac12 = \\frac18</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(B_1\\mid R) = \\frac{3/8}{3/8 + 1/8} = \\frac34</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A factory's machines A, B and C produce 50%, 30% and 20% of items, with defect rates 1%, 2% and 3%. A randomly chosen item is defective. What is the probability it came from machine C?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Weights: A <span class=\"m\">0.5\\times0.01 = 0.005</span>, B <span class=\"m\">0.3\\times0.02 = 0.006</span>, C <span class=\"m\">0.2\\times0.03 = 0.006</span>. Total <span class=\"m\">P(D) = 0.017</span>. So <span class=\"m\">P(C\\mid D) = \\frac{0.006}{0.017} = \\frac{6}{17}\\approx0.353</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Bayesian machine learning treats model parameters as random: <span class=\"m\">p(\\theta\\mid\\text{data})\\propto p(\\text{data}\\mid\\theta)\\,p(\\theta)</span>. Regularization is a prior in disguise: an L2 penalty corresponds to a Gaussian prior on the weights and an L1 penalty to a Laplace prior. Naive Bayes spam filters apply Bayes' rule over words, and a classifier calibrated on balanced data must be adjusted with the true class prior before deployment on imbalanced data.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Bayesian updating runs inside many systems: robot localization (particle filters), GPS sensor fusion (Kalman filters), A/B testing dashboards, and fraud detection, where each new transaction updates the probability that an account is compromised.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Courts, doctors and journalists misuse conditional probabilities often. In the 1999 Sally Clark case in the UK, an expert's claim that two cot deaths in one family had a \"1 in 73 million\" chance was presented as if it were the probability of innocence. Ignoring the alternative explanation's own rarity, a Bayes error, contributed to a wrongful conviction later overturned.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">P(E\\mid H) = 0.8</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(H) = 0.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(E) = 0.6</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">P(H\\mid E)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2/3|0.667|0.6667\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\frac{0.8\\times0.5}{0.6} = \\frac{0.4}{0.6} = \\frac23</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In Bayes' theorem, <span class=\"m\">P(H)</span> is called the...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">prior</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">likelihood</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">posterior</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It's the belief before seeing the evidence.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A test has sensitivity 0.9 and false-positive rate 0.1, and the condition has prevalence 0.5. Find <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{condition}\\mid +)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.9|9/10\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\frac{0.9\\times0.5}{0.9\\times0.5 + 0.1\\times0.5} = \\frac{0.45}{0.5} = 0.9</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why do rare diseases produce many false positives even with accurate tests?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Healthy people vastly outnumber sick people, so a small false-positive rate still yields many positives</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Tests are less accurate for rare diseases</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Bayes' theorem doesn't apply to rare events</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>10% of 9,900 healthy people (990) is far more than 95% of 100 sick people (95).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Prior odds are 1:4 and the likelihood ratio is 8. What is the posterior probability of <span class=\"m\">H</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2/3|0.667|0.6667\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Posterior odds <span class=\"m\">8\\times\\frac14 = 2</span>, i.e. 2:1, so probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac{2}{3}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Two independent positive tests each have likelihood ratio 9.5, and the prior odds are 1:99. What are the posterior odds? (Round to 2 decimals.)</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.91|0.912\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Each test multiplies the odds by 9.5: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{99}\\times9.5\\times9.5 = \\frac{90.25}{99}\\approx0.91</span>, a probability of about 0.48.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "discrete-rv": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"from-outcomes-to-numbers\" data-h=\"3\">From outcomes to numbers</h3>\n<p>So far we've described random experiments by their outcomes: \"heads, tails, heads,\" \"the ace of spades,\" \"a 4.\" But most questions we care about are about <em>numbers</em>. How many heads in ten flips? How much money will I win? How many customers will arrive in the next hour? A <strong>random variable</strong> is the tool that turns outcomes into numbers so we can do arithmetic with uncertainty.</p>\n<p>Formally, a random variable <span class=\"m\">X</span> is a function that assigns a real number to every outcome in the sample space. Flip a coin three times. The sample space has 8 outcomes such as HHT and TTT. Let <span class=\"m\">X</span> be the number of heads. Then <span class=\"m\">X(\\text{HHT}) = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">X(\\text{TTT}) = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">X(\\text{HHH}) = 3</span>. The name is a little misleading: a random variable isn't a variable in the algebra sense and it isn't itself random. It is a fixed rule; the randomness comes from which outcome the experiment produces.</p>\n<p>We use capital letters like <span class=\"m\">X</span> for the random variable and lowercase like <span class=\"m\">x</span> for a particular value it might take. The statement \"<span class=\"m\">X = 2</span>\" is an <em>event</em>: the set of outcomes where the rule gives 2, here <span class=\"m\">\\{\\text{HHT}, \\text{HTH}, \\text{THH}\\}</span>. So <span class=\"m\">P(X = 2) = \\frac38</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Random variable</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A function <span class=\"m\">X</span> from the sample space to the real numbers. It turns each outcome into a number.</p></div></div>\n<p>A random variable is <strong>discrete</strong> if it takes a finite or countable list of values, such as <span class=\"m\">0, 1, 2, 3</span> or all whole numbers <span class=\"m\">0, 1, 2, \\dots</span>. Counts are the typical example. (The next lessons treat <strong>continuous</strong> random variables, like heights and waiting times, which can take any value in an interval.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-probability-mass-function\" data-h=\"3\">The probability mass function</h3>\n<p>The complete description of a discrete random variable is its <strong>probability mass function</strong> (PMF), <span class=\"m\">p(x) = P(X = x)</span>, which lists the probability of each possible value. For the three coin flips:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><span class=\"m\">x</span></th>\n<th>0</th>\n<th>1</th>\n<th>2</th>\n<th>3</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"m\">p(x)</span></td>\n<td>1/8</td>\n<td>3/8</td>\n<td>3/8</td>\n<td>1/8</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Two rules always hold. Every value satisfies <span class=\"m\">p(x)\\ge0</span>, and the probabilities add to one: <span class=\"m\">\\sum_x p(x) = 1</span>. Here <span class=\"m\">\\frac18 + \\frac38 + \\frac38 + \\frac18 = 1</span>. Any table of non-negative numbers summing to 1 is a valid PMF. To find the probability of a set of values, add their masses: <span class=\"m\">P(X\\ge2) = \\frac38 + \\frac18 = \\frac12</span>. We draw a PMF as a bar chart: one bar per value, with heights equal to probabilities.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Probability mass function (PMF)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a discrete random variable, <span class=\"m\">p(x) = P(X = x)</span>. It satisfies <span class=\"m\">p(x)\\ge0</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\sum_x p(x) = 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-cumulative-distribution-function\" data-h=\"3\">The cumulative distribution function</h3>\n<p>Often we want \"at most\" probabilities. The <strong>cumulative distribution function</strong> (CDF) is <span class=\"m\">F(x) = P(X\\le x)</span>. For the coins, <span class=\"m\">F(0) = \\frac18</span>, <span class=\"m\">F(1) = \\frac48</span>, <span class=\"m\">F(2) = \\frac78</span> and <span class=\"m\">F(3) = 1</span>. Between these values the CDF stays flat, so its graph is a staircase that jumps at each possible value by the height of that value's bar. A CDF always rises from 0 to 1 and never decreases. It's useful because interval probabilities become subtractions: <span class=\"m\">P(a &lt; X\\le b) = F(b) - F(a)</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Cumulative distribution function (CDF)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">F(x) = P(X\\le x)</span>. It is non-decreasing, starts at 0 and ends at 1, and works for every type of random variable.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"expected-value-the-long-run-average\" data-h=\"3\">Expected value: the long-run average</h3>\n<p>Imagine playing a game many times: roll a die and win the number shown in dollars. What do you win on average? Each face appears about <span class=\"m\">\\frac16</span> of the time, so the average is <span class=\"m\">1\\cdot\\frac16 + 2\\cdot\\frac16 + \\dots + 6\\cdot\\frac16 = \\frac{21}{6} = 3.5</span>. This is the <strong>expected value</strong> or <strong>mean</strong>:\n<span class=\"mb\">E[X] = \\sum_x x\\,p(x).</span>\nIt is a weighted average of the values, with probabilities as weights. Picture the bar chart as a set of weights on a ruler; the expected value is the point where the ruler balances. Notice that <span class=\"m\">E[X] = 3.5</span> is not a value the die can ever show. The expected value is a long-run average, not the most likely result. For three coin flips, <span class=\"m\">E[X] = 0\\cdot\\frac18 + 1\\cdot\\frac38 + 2\\cdot\\frac38 + 3\\cdot\\frac18 = \\frac{12}{8} = 1.5</span>, which matches the intuition \"half of three flips.\"</p>\n<p>Expected values are how we decide whether bets are fair. A raffle ticket costs &#36;2, and there's a <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{1000}</span> chance of winning &#36;1,000. Your net gain <span class=\"m\">G</span> is &#36;998 with probability 0.001 and $-&#36;2$ otherwise, so <span class=\"m\">E[G] = 998(0.001) - 2(0.999) = 0.998 - 1.998 = -1</span>. On average you lose a dollar per ticket.</p>\n<h3 id=\"expected-value-of-a-function\" data-h=\"3\">Expected value of a function</h3>\n<p>If you want the average of some function of <span class=\"m\">X</span>, such as <span class=\"m\">X^2</span> or a payoff <span class=\"m\">g(X)</span>, you don't need to work out the distribution of <span class=\"m\">g(X)</span> first. Just weight <span class=\"m\">g(x)</span> by <span class=\"m\">p(x)</span>: <span class=\"m\">E[g(X)] = \\sum_x g(x)\\,p(x)</span>. This is sometimes called the <em>law of the unconscious statistician</em>. For a die, <span class=\"m\">E[X^2] = \\frac{1 + 4 + 9 + 16 + 25 + 36}{6} = \\frac{91}{6}</span>. Be careful: <span class=\"m\">E[X^2]</span> is not <span class=\"m\">(E[X])^2 = 12.25</span>. In general <span class=\"m\">E[g(X)]\\ne g(E[X])</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"variance-how-spread-out\" data-h=\"3\">Variance: how spread out</h3>\n<p>Two games can have the same mean but feel very different. Game A always pays &#36;5. Game B pays &#36;0 or &#36;10 with equal chance. Both have mean 5, but B is riskier. The <strong>variance</strong> measures how far values typically land from the mean:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{Var}(X) = E\\big[(X - \\mu)^2\\big],\\quad \\mu = E[X].</span>\nWe square deviations so that values above and below the mean don't cancel, and so that big deviations count more. For game A the variance is 0. For game B, <span class=\"m\">(0 - 5)^2\\cdot\\frac12 + (10 - 5)^2\\cdot\\frac12 = 25</span>.</p>\n<p>There's a shortcut that's usually quicker: <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X) = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2</span>. For a die, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{91}{6} - 3.5^2 = \\frac{91}{6} - \\frac{49}{4} = \\frac{182 - 147}{12} = \\frac{35}{12}\\approx2.92</span>.</p>\n<p>Because variance is in squared units (dollars squared), we often report the <strong>standard deviation</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = \\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var}(X)}</span>, which is back in the original units. Game B has <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 5</span>: values typically land about &#36;5 from the mean.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Expected value</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">E[X] = \\sum_x x\\,p(x)</span>: the probability-weighted average, the balance point of the distribution.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Variance and standard deviation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X) = E[(X - \\mu)^2] = E[X^2] - \\mu^2</span>; the standard deviation is <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = \\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var}(X)}</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Don't confuse the random variable with its values: <span class=\"m\">X</span> is the rule, <span class=\"m\">x</span> is a number. Don't forget to check that a PMF adds to 1; if a problem gives probabilities with an unknown <span class=\"m\">c</span>, that's how you solve for <span class=\"m\">c</span>. Remember that the expected value need not be a possible value and need not be the most likely value. When using the shortcut formula, square <em>after</em> averaging for the second term: it's <span class=\"m\">E[X^2] - (E[X])^2</span>, and the result must never be negative. Finally, standard deviation is the square root of variance, not the average absolute deviation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A random variable turns outcomes into numbers. A discrete one is described by its PMF <span class=\"m\">p(x)</span>, which is non-negative and sums to 1, or equivalently by its staircase CDF <span class=\"m\">F(x) = P(X\\le x)</span>. The expected value <span class=\"m\">E[X] = \\sum x\\,p(x)</span> is the long-run average and balance point, and the variance <span class=\"m\">E[X^2] - \\mu^2</span> measures spread, with standard deviation as its square root.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"distribution\" data-args='{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;binomial&quot;, &quot;lo&quot;: 2, &quot;hi&quot;: 3}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Three coin flips is a binomial with n = 3. Set n = 3 to match the table in the article.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Set <span class=\"m\">n = 3</span> and <span class=\"m\">p = 0.5</span>. Check that the bars have heights 1/8, 3/8, 3/8, 1/8 and that the mean reads 1.5.\n2. Shade from 2 to 3 and confirm <span class=\"m\">P(2\\le X\\le3) = 0.5</span>, matching <span class=\"m\">P(X\\ge2)</span> from the article.\n3. Change <span class=\"m\">p</span> to 0.8. Watch the bars and the dashed mean line slide to the right.\n4. Switch to \"geometric\" and move <span class=\"m\">p</span>. The mean <span class=\"m\">1/p</span> grows quickly as <span class=\"m\">p</span> gets small.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the dashed line is the balance point of the bars: heavier bars on one side pull it that way. The variance readout is largest when the bars are spread out and shrinks when the mass piles onto a few values.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">p(x) = P(X = x),\\quad p(x)\\ge0,\\quad \\sum_x p(x) = 1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">F(x) = P(X\\le x) = \\sum_{t\\le x}p(t)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">E[X] = \\sum_x x\\,p(x),\\qquad E[g(X)] = \\sum_x g(x)p(x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{Var}(X) = E[(X-\\mu)^2] = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sigma_X = \\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var}(X)}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Shortcut formula for variance</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X) = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">\\mu = E[X]</span>. Expanding, <span class=\"m\">E[(X-\\mu)^2] = \\sum_x(x^2 - 2\\mu x + \\mu^2)p(x) = \\sum x^2p(x) - 2\\mu\\sum xp(x) + \\mu^2\\sum p(x) = E[X^2] - 2\\mu^2 + \\mu^2 = E[X^2] - \\mu^2</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A PMF is p(1) = c, p(2) = 2c, p(3) = 3c. Find c, E[X] and Var(X).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Sum to one: <span class=\"m\">c + 2c + 3c = 6c = 1</span>, so <span class=\"m\">c = \\frac16</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">E[X] = 1\\cdot\\frac16 + 2\\cdot\\frac26 + 3\\cdot\\frac36 = \\frac{14}{6} = \\frac73</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">E[X^2] = \\frac16 + \\frac{8}{6} + \\frac{27}{6} = \\frac{36}{6} = 6</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X) = 6 - \\frac{49}{9} = \\frac{5}{9}</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A game: roll a die; win &#36;6 on a six, lose &#36;1 otherwise. Is it fair?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">E[\\text{gain}] = 6\\cdot\\frac16 + (-1)\\cdot\\frac56 = \\frac{6 - 5}{6} = \\frac16</span>.</li>\n<li>The expected gain is positive (about 17 cents), so the game favours the player.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">For X = number of heads in 3 fair flips, find F(1) and P(1 &lt; X ≤ 3).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">F(1) = p(0) + p(1) = \\frac18 + \\frac38 = \\frac12</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(1 &lt; X\\le3) = F(3) - F(1) = 1 - \\frac12 = \\frac12</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An insurer sells a one-year policy for &#36;200. With probability 0.01 the customer makes a claim of &#36;10,000; otherwise nothing is paid. Find the insurer's expected profit and the standard deviation of its profit on one policy.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Profit is <span class=\"m\">200 - 10000 = -9800</span> with probability 0.01 and <span class=\"m\">200</span> with probability 0.99. <span class=\"m\">E = -9800(0.01) + 200(0.99) = -98 + 198 = 100</span>. <span class=\"m\">E[\\text{profit}^2] = 9800^2(0.01) + 200^2(0.99) = 960400 + 39600 = 1000000</span>. Variance <span class=\"m\">= 1000000 - 100^2 = 990000</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = \\sqrt{990000}\\approx995</span>. Expected profit is &#36;100 but with a large spread, which is why insurers pool many policies.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A classifier's output is a PMF over classes: a softmax layer produces non-negative numbers that sum to one. The training loss is an expected value over the data distribution, <span class=\"m\">E[\\ell(f(x), y)]</span>, which we estimate by averaging over a sample. Reinforcement learning is built on expected rewards: an agent picks actions to maximize <span class=\"m\">E[\\text{return}]</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The average-case running time of an algorithm is an expected value over random inputs or random choices. Quicksort with random pivots makes about <span class=\"m\">2n\\ln n</span> comparisons on average, even though its worst case is <span class=\"m\">\\binom n2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Casinos, lotteries and insurers all live on expected value. A US roulette bet on red pays even money with probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac{18}{38}</span>, so the expected gain per &#36;1 is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{18}{38} - \\frac{20}{38} = -\\frac{2}{38}\\approx-5.3</span> cents. Over millions of bets, that average becomes the casino's guaranteed profit.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A fair die is rolled. What is <span class=\"m\">E[X]</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3.5|7/2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(1+2+3+4+5+6)/6 = 21/6</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">X</span> takes the values 0 and 10 with probability 1/2 each. Find <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"25\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Mean 5; each deviation squared is 25.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">p(0) = 0.2</span>, <span class=\"m\">p(1) = 0.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">p(2) = c</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">c</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.3|3/10\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The masses must sum to 1.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">With the PMF above (<span class=\"m\">c = 0.3</span>), find <span class=\"m\">E[X]</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1.1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">0(0.2) + 1(0.5) + 2(0.3) = 1.1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which statement about <span class=\"m\">E[X]</span> is always true?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">It is the most likely value of <span class=\"m\">X</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">It is the probability-weighted average of the values</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">It is a value <span class=\"m\">X</span> can take</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A die has mean 3.5, which it can never show.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">E[X] = 2</span> and <span class=\"m\">E[X^2] = 7</span>. Find the standard deviation of <span class=\"m\">X</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1.732|1.73\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X) = 7 - 4 = 3</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = \\sqrt3\\approx1.732</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "expectation-properties": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-we-need-rules\" data-h=\"3\">Why we need rules</h3>\n<p>Computing an expected value straight from the definition means listing every value and its probability. That's fine for one die, but what about the number of heads in 100 flips, or the number of people in a room of 30 who share a birthday with someone else? The distributions are messy. Fortunately, expectation and variance obey a handful of simple algebraic rules, and with them we can find means and spreads of complicated quantities without ever writing down their distributions. These rules are used constantly in statistics and machine learning, so it pays to know them by heart and to know <em>why</em> they hold.</p>\n<h3 id=\"shifting-and-scaling\" data-h=\"3\">Shifting and scaling</h3>\n<p>Suppose <span class=\"m\">X</span> is a temperature in Celsius with <span class=\"m\">E[X] = 20</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X) = 4</span>. In Fahrenheit the temperature is <span class=\"m\">Y = 1.8X + 32</span>. What are the mean and variance of <span class=\"m\">Y</span>?</p>\n<p>The mean simply follows the formula: <span class=\"m\">E[aX + b] = aE[X] + b</span>. So <span class=\"m\">E[Y] = 1.8(20) + 32 = 68</span>. This makes sense: if every value is scaled by 1.8 and shifted by 32, so is their weighted average. Algebraically, <span class=\"m\">\\sum(ax + b)p(x) = a\\sum xp(x) + b\\sum p(x) = aE[X] + b</span>.</p>\n<p>The variance behaves differently: <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(aX + b) = a^2\\operatorname{Var}(X)</span>. Adding the constant 32 moves every value and the mean together, so deviations from the mean don't change at all. Scaling by 1.8 scales every deviation by 1.8, and squaring turns that into <span class=\"m\">1.8^2 = 3.24</span>. So <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(Y) = 3.24\\times4 = 12.96</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{SD}(Y) = 1.8\\times2 = 3.6</span>. Note the standard deviation scales by <span class=\"m\">|a|</span>, never by a negative number: <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(-X) = \\operatorname{Var}(X)</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"linearity-of-expectation\" data-h=\"3\">Linearity of expectation</h3>\n<p>The single most useful rule in probability is that <strong>the expectation of a sum is the sum of the expectations</strong>:\n<span class=\"mb\">E[X + Y] = E[X] + E[Y].</span>\nThis holds <em>always</em>, whether or not <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> are independent. Roll two dice. The mean of each is 3.5, so the mean of the total is 7, with no need to list all 36 outcomes. Extend it to any number of terms: <span class=\"m\">E[X_1 + \\dots + X_n] = E[X_1] + \\dots + E[X_n]</span>. The average of <span class=\"m\">n</span> dice totals <span class=\"m\">3.5n</span>.</p>\n<p>Why is it true even for dependent variables? Write it using the joint probabilities: <span class=\"m\">E[X + Y] = \\sum_{x,y}(x + y)P(X = x, Y = y)</span>. Split into <span class=\"m\">\\sum_{x,y}x\\,P(x, y) + \\sum_{x,y}y\\,P(x, y)</span>. Summing the joint probability over <span class=\"m\">y</span> gives <span class=\"m\">P(X = x)</span>, so the first part is <span class=\"m\">E[X]</span>, and similarly the second is <span class=\"m\">E[Y]</span>. Dependence never entered the argument.</p>\n<h3 id=\"indicator-variables-counting-with-linearity\" data-h=\"3\">Indicator variables: counting with linearity</h3>\n<p>An <strong>indicator variable</strong> <span class=\"m\">I_A</span> equals 1 if the event <span class=\"m\">A</span> happens and 0 if it doesn't. Its expected value is <span class=\"m\">E[I_A] = 1\\cdot P(A) + 0\\cdot P(A^c) = P(A)</span>. This little fact combined with linearity is a superpower for counting problems.</p>\n<p>How many heads do you expect in 100 flips of a coin that lands heads with probability <span class=\"m\">p</span>? Let <span class=\"m\">I_k</span> indicate \"flip <span class=\"m\">k</span> is heads.\" The number of heads is <span class=\"m\">I_1 + \\dots + I_{100}</span>, so its expectation is <span class=\"m\">100p</span>.</p>\n<p>Here's a harder one. Ten people throw their hats in a pile, and each grabs a hat at random. How many people expect to get their own hat back? Let <span class=\"m\">I_k</span> indicate \"person <span class=\"m\">k</span> gets their own hat.\" Each person is equally likely to grab any of the 10 hats, so <span class=\"m\">P(I_k = 1) = \\frac{1}{10}</span>. The expected number of matches is <span class=\"m\">10\\times\\frac{1}{10} = 1</span>. The indicators are dependent (if nine people match, the tenth must too), but linearity doesn't care. Remarkably, the answer is 1 no matter how many people there are.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Indicator variable</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">I_A = 1</span> if event <span class=\"m\">A</span> occurs and 0 otherwise. Its expectation is <span class=\"m\">E[I_A] = P(A)</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"variance-of-a-sum-and-covariance\" data-h=\"3\">Variance of a sum and covariance</h3>\n<p>Variance does <em>not</em> always add. If <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> tend to move together, their sum swings more widely; if they move in opposite directions, the swings partly cancel. The precise statement uses the <strong>covariance</strong>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{Cov}(X, Y) = E[(X - \\mu_X)(Y - \\mu_Y)] = E[XY] - E[X]E[Y].</span>\nCovariance is positive when <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> tend to be above their means at the same time and negative when one is above while the other is below. Then\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{Var}(X + Y) = \\operatorname{Var}(X) + \\operatorname{Var}(Y) + 2\\operatorname{Cov}(X, Y).</span>\nAn extreme example: if <span class=\"m\">Y = -X</span>, then <span class=\"m\">X + Y = 0</span> has variance 0, and indeed <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Cov}(X, -X) = -\\operatorname{Var}(X)</span> cancels the two variances.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Covariance</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Cov}(X, Y) = E[(X - \\mu_X)(Y - \\mu_Y)] = E[XY] - E[X]E[Y]</span>; it measures how two variables move together.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"independence-makes-variances-add\" data-h=\"3\">Independence makes variances add</h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> are independent, then <span class=\"m\">E[XY] = E[X]E[Y]</span>, so their covariance is 0 and\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{Var}(X + Y) = \\operatorname{Var}(X) + \\operatorname{Var}(Y).</span>\nThe total of two independent dice has variance <span class=\"m\">\\frac{35}{12} + \\frac{35}{12} = \\frac{35}{6}</span>. More generally, for independent <span class=\"m\">X_1,\\dots,X_n</span> with common variance <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2</span>, the sum has variance <span class=\"m\">n\\sigma^2</span>, and the <strong>average</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\bar X = \\frac1n\\sum X_i</span> has variance <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{n^2}\\cdot n\\sigma^2 = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}</span>. Its standard deviation is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}</span>. This one line is why averaging many measurements reduces noise, and it is the seed of the law of large numbers and of every confidence interval in statistics.</p>\n<p>Notice the difference: <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X + X) = \\operatorname{Var}(2X) = 4\\operatorname{Var}(X)</span>, but the sum of two <em>independent copies</em> has variance <span class=\"m\">2\\operatorname{Var}(X)</span>. Doubling one bet is riskier than making two independent bets of the same size.</p>\n<h3 id=\"expectations-of-functions-careful-with-nonlinear-ones\" data-h=\"3\">Expectations of functions: careful with nonlinear ones</h3>\n<p>To find the expected value of a function <span class=\"m\">g(X)</span> we don't need the distribution of <span class=\"m\">g(X)</span> itself; we can weight each value <span class=\"m\">g(x)</span> by the probability of <span class=\"m\">x</span>: <span class=\"m\">E[g(X)] = \\sum_x g(x)P(X = x)</span>. Statisticians jokingly call this the law of the unconscious statistician, because it's so natural that people use it without noticing. The important warning is that expectation passes through <em>linear</em> functions but not through nonlinear ones. Let <span class=\"m\">X</span> be equally likely to be 1, 2 or 3. Then <span class=\"m\">E[X] = 2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">(E[X])^2 = 4</span>, but <span class=\"m\">E[X^2] = \\frac{1 + 4 + 9}{3} = \\frac{14}{3}\\approx4.67</span>. The gap, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{14}{3} - 4 = \\frac23</span>, is exactly the variance. In general, for a convex function such as <span class=\"m\">x^2</span> or <span class=\"m\">e^x</span>, <strong>Jensen's inequality</strong> says <span class=\"m\">E[g(X)]\\ge g(E[X])</span>. This is why the average of squared errors is never smaller than the square of the average error, and why plugging an average input into a nonlinear formula usually gives the wrong average output.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The most common error is writing <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X - Y) = \\operatorname{Var}(X) - \\operatorname{Var}(Y)</span>. For independent variables, variances add even when you subtract: <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X - Y) = \\operatorname{Var}(X) + \\operatorname{Var}(Y)</span>, because <span class=\"m\">-Y</span> has the same variance as <span class=\"m\">Y</span>. Another mistake is assuming <span class=\"m\">E[XY] = E[X]E[Y]</span> without independence, or assuming zero covariance means independence; it doesn't, as the next lesson on joint distributions shows. Finally, remember <span class=\"m\">E[g(X)]\\ne g(E[X])</span> for non-linear <span class=\"m\">g</span>: linearity is only for linear combinations.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Expectation is linear: <span class=\"m\">E[aX + bY + c] = aE[X] + bE[Y] + c</span>, always. Indicators turn counting into adding probabilities. Variance ignores shifts and scales by <span class=\"m\">a^2</span>. Variances of sums add the covariance term, <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X + Y) = \\operatorname{Var}(X) + \\operatorname{Var}(Y) + 2\\operatorname{Cov}(X, Y)</span>, which vanishes under independence, so the mean of <span class=\"m\">n</span> independent values has variance <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2/n</span>.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"lln\" data-args='{&quot;p&quot;: 0.5}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Each flip is an indicator with mean p. The running average of n flips has standard deviation sqrt(p(1-p)/n).</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Press \"Flip 1000 × 5 runs\" a few times with <span class=\"m\">p = 0.5</span>. Look at how far apart the five running averages are after 10 flips and after 1000 flips.\n2. Set <span class=\"m\">p = 0.9</span> and repeat. The curves settle near 0.9, and they wobble less than at <span class=\"m\">p = 0.5</span>.\n3. Set <span class=\"m\">p = 0.05</span>. The spread is again smaller than at 0.5.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the running average is <span class=\"m\">\\bar X = \\frac1n\\sum I_k</span>, so by linearity its mean is <span class=\"m\">p</span>, and by independence its variance is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}</span>. That's why the five runs pinch together like <span class=\"m\">1/\\sqrt n</span>, and why <span class=\"m\">p(1-p)</span>, largest at <span class=\"m\">p = 0.5</span>, controls how wide the funnel is.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">E[aX + b] = aE[X] + b,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(aX + b) = a^2\\operatorname{Var}(X)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">E\\Big[\\sum_i a_iX_i\\Big] = \\sum_i a_iE[X_i]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">E[I_A] = P(A)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y) = E[XY] - E[X]E[Y]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{Var}(X \\pm Y) = \\operatorname{Var}(X) + \\operatorname{Var}(Y) \\pm 2\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">X_i \\text{ i.i.d.}: \\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X) = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Linearity of expectation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any random variables <span class=\"m\">X, Y</span> with finite means and constants <span class=\"m\">a, b</span>: <span class=\"m\">E[aX + bY] = aE[X] + bE[Y]</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">E[aX + bY] = \\sum_{x,y}(ax + by)P(X=x, Y=y) = a\\sum_x x\\sum_y P(x, y) + b\\sum_y y\\sum_x P(x, y) = a\\sum_x xP(X = x) + b\\sum_y yP(Y = y)</span>, using the fact that summing a joint PMF over one variable gives the other's marginal PMF.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Variance of a sum</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X + Y) = \\operatorname{Var}(X) + \\operatorname{Var}(Y) + 2\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">D_X = X - \\mu_X</span> and <span class=\"m\">D_Y = Y - \\mu_Y</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">X + Y - (\\mu_X + \\mu_Y) = D_X + D_Y</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X+Y) = E[(D_X + D_Y)^2] = E[D_X^2] + E[D_Y^2] + 2E[D_XD_Y]</span>, which are the three terms.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">X has mean 3 and variance 4. Find E[5 − 2X] and Var(5 − 2X).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">E[5 - 2X] = 5 - 2(3) = -1</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(5 - 2X) = (-2)^2\\cdot4 = 16</span>; the constant 5 doesn't matter.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Roll a die 12 times. What is the expected number of sixes, and of distinct faces that appear?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Sixes: indicators with <span class=\"m\">P = \\frac16</span>, so <span class=\"m\">12\\times\\frac16 = 2</span>.</li>\n<li>Distinct faces: let <span class=\"m\">J_f</span> indicate \"face <span class=\"m\">f</span> appears at least once\": <span class=\"m\">P = 1 - (\\frac56)^{12}\\approx1 - 0.112 = 0.888</span>.</li>\n<li>Expected distinct faces <span class=\"m\">= 6\\times0.888\\approx5.33</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">X and Y are independent with SDs 3 and 4. Find SD(X − Y).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X - Y) = 9 + 16 = 25</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{SD}(X - Y) = 5</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A portfolio puts &#36;1 in each of two stocks. Each stock's one-year return has mean 0.08 and standard deviation 0.20. Find the mean and standard deviation of the total return (a) if the returns are independent, and (b) if their covariance is 0.02.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>The mean is <span class=\"m\">0.08 + 0.08 = 0.16</span> in both cases. (a) <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var} = 0.04 + 0.04 = 0.08</span>, SD <span class=\"m\">= \\sqrt{0.08}\\approx0.283</span>. (b) <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var} = 0.04 + 0.04 + 2(0.02) = 0.12</span>, SD <span class=\"m\">\\approx0.346</span>. Positive covariance reduces the benefit of diversification.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Mini-batch stochastic gradient descent relies on these rules. The gradient on a random batch of <span class=\"m\">B</span> examples is an average, so by linearity its expectation is the full gradient (it's unbiased), and by independence its variance is about <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2/B</span>. Larger batches give less noisy steps, with diminishing returns like <span class=\"m\">1/\\sqrt B</span>. Ensembles average models for the same reason; correlated models (positive covariance) reduce variance less.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Linearity with indicators analyses randomized algorithms: the expected number of collisions when hashing <span class=\"m\">n</span> keys into <span class=\"m\">m</span> slots is <span class=\"m\">\\binom n2\\frac1m</span>, since each pair collides with probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac1m</span>. Randomized quicksort's <span class=\"m\">O(n\\log n)</span> average comes from summing indicator variables \"elements <span class=\"m\">i</span> and <span class=\"m\">j</span> are compared.\"</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Measurement labs repeat readings and report the average because its standard deviation is <span class=\"m\">\\sigma/\\sqrt n</span>: four readings halve the noise. Financial advisers diversify for the same reason, while warning that stocks in the same sector have positive covariance and protect each other less.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">E[X] = 4</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">E[3X - 2]</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"10\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">3(4) - 2 = 10</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X) = 5</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(-2X + 7)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"20\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(-2)^2\\times5 = 20</span>; the shift doesn't matter.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">X</span>, <span class=\"m\">Y</span> are independent with variances 2 and 3. Find <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X - Y)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Variances add for independent variables, even when subtracting.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Eight people pick hats at random. What is the expected number who get their own hat?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">8\\times\\frac18 = 1</span> by linearity with indicators.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Does <span class=\"m\">E[X + Y] = E[X] + E[Y]</span> require independence?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No, it always holds</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Linearity of expectation holds for any random variables with finite means.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Single measurements have SD 6. What is the SD of the average of 9 independent measurements?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">6/\\sqrt9 = 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "discrete-dists": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"patterns-that-keep-coming-back\" data-h=\"3\">Patterns that keep coming back</h3>\n<p>A quality inspector checks 20 bolts and counts the defective ones. A basketball player takes free throws until she misses. A call center counts the calls that arrive in a minute. These look like different problems, but each of them follows a pattern that statisticians have studied so thoroughly that its PMF, mean and variance are known formulas. Learning to recognise these <strong>named distributions</strong> saves an enormous amount of work: once you see \"count of successes in a fixed number of independent trials,\" you immediately know the answer is binomial, and you can write down any probability you need.</p>\n<p>This lesson covers the five discrete distributions you'll meet most often. For each one we'll ask: what story produces it, what is its PMF, and what are its mean and variance?</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-bernoulli-distribution-one-yes-or-no-trial\" data-h=\"3\">The Bernoulli distribution: one yes-or-no trial</h3>\n<p>The simplest random variable takes the value 1 (\"success\") with probability <span class=\"m\">p</span> and 0 (\"failure\") with probability <span class=\"m\">1 - p</span>. A single coin flip, a single email being spam or not, a single customer clicking an ad or not: each is a <strong>Bernoulli trial</strong>. We write <span class=\"m\">X\\sim\\text{Bernoulli}(p)</span>.</p>\n<p>The mean is <span class=\"m\">E[X] = 1\\cdot p + 0\\cdot(1 - p) = p</span>. Since <span class=\"m\">X^2 = X</span> (both 0 and 1 are their own squares), <span class=\"m\">E[X^2] = p</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X) = p - p^2 = p(1 - p)</span>. The variance is largest at <span class=\"m\">p = \\frac12</span>, where the outcome is most uncertain, and zero at <span class=\"m\">p = 0</span> or <span class=\"m\">1</span>, where there's no uncertainty at all.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Bernoulli distribution</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A single trial with <span class=\"m\">P(X = 1) = p</span> and <span class=\"m\">P(X = 0) = 1 - p</span>. Mean <span class=\"m\">p</span>, variance <span class=\"m\">p(1-p)</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-binomial-distribution-counting-successes\" data-h=\"3\">The binomial distribution: counting successes</h3>\n<p>Now repeat a Bernoulli trial <span class=\"m\">n</span> times, independently, with the same <span class=\"m\">p</span> each time, and count the successes. That count <span class=\"m\">X</span> has a <strong>binomial distribution</strong>, <span class=\"m\">X\\sim\\text{Bin}(n, p)</span>. To find <span class=\"m\">P(X = k)</span>, think about one particular sequence with <span class=\"m\">k</span> successes and <span class=\"m\">n - k</span> failures, say SSFF...F. By independence its probability is <span class=\"m\">p^k(1 - p)^{n-k}</span>. Every other arrangement of <span class=\"m\">k</span> successes has the same probability, and there are <span class=\"m\">\\binom nk</span> arrangements, so\n<span class=\"mb\">P(X = k) = \\binom nk p^k(1 - p)^{n-k},\\qquad k = 0, 1, \\dots, n.</span>\nFor example, a fair coin flipped 10 times gives exactly 5 heads with probability <span class=\"m\">\\binom{10}{5}\\frac{1}{2^{10}} = \\frac{252}{1024}\\approx0.246</span>. Even the most likely result happens less than a quarter of the time.</p>\n<p>The mean and variance follow beautifully from the last lesson. <span class=\"m\">X</span> is a sum of <span class=\"m\">n</span> independent Bernoulli(<span class=\"m\">p</span>) indicators, so <span class=\"m\">E[X] = np</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X) = np(1 - p)</span>. Ten fair flips have mean 5 and standard deviation <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{2.5}\\approx1.58</span>.</p>\n<p>Check the conditions before using the binomial: a <strong>fixed</strong> number of trials, each with <strong>two</strong> outcomes, the <strong>same</strong> probability of success each time, and <strong>independent</strong> trials. Drawing 5 cards from a deck and counting hearts fails independence (no replacement), so it's not exactly binomial; that situation is called hypergeometric.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Binomial distribution</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The number of successes in <span class=\"m\">n</span> independent trials each with success probability <span class=\"m\">p</span>: <span class=\"m\">P(X=k) = \\binom nk p^k(1-p)^{n-k}</span>, mean <span class=\"m\">np</span>, variance <span class=\"m\">np(1-p)</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-geometric-distribution-waiting-for-the-first-success\" data-h=\"3\">The geometric distribution: waiting for the first success</h3>\n<p>Instead of fixing the number of trials, keep going until the first success and let <span class=\"m\">X</span> be the number of trials needed. For the first success to occur on trial <span class=\"m\">k</span>, the first <span class=\"m\">k - 1</span> trials must fail and the <span class=\"m\">k</span>-th must succeed:\n<span class=\"mb\">P(X = k) = (1 - p)^{k-1}p,\\qquad k = 1, 2, 3, \\dots</span>\nIf you roll a die until a six appears, <span class=\"m\">P(X = 3) = (\\frac56)^2\\frac16 = \\frac{25}{216}\\approx0.116</span>. The mean is <span class=\"m\">E[X] = \\frac1p</span>: on average it takes 6 rolls to see a six. The variance is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1 - p}{p^2}</span>, which is 30 for the die, so the waiting time is very variable.</p>\n<p>The geometric distribution is <strong>memoryless</strong>: if you've already failed 10 times, the number of further trials you'll need has the same distribution as when you started. The die doesn't remember your bad luck. Formally <span class=\"m\">P(X &gt; m + k\\mid X &gt; m) = P(X &gt; k)</span>, because <span class=\"m\">P(X &gt; k) = (1 - p)^k</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Geometric distribution</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The trial number of the first success in independent trials: <span class=\"m\">P(X = k) = (1-p)^{k-1}p</span> for <span class=\"m\">k\\ge1</span>, mean <span class=\"m\">1/p</span>, variance <span class=\"m\">(1-p)/p^2</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-poisson-distribution-counting-rare-events\" data-h=\"3\">The Poisson distribution: counting rare events</h3>\n<p>Many counts have no natural \"number of trials\": emails per hour, typos per page, earthquakes per year. Events happen at some average rate <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> in an interval, independently of each other. The count has a <strong>Poisson distribution</strong>:\n<span class=\"mb\">P(X = k) = \\frac{\\lambda^k e^{-\\lambda}}{k!},\\qquad k = 0, 1, 2, \\dots</span>\nIf a help desk gets on average 3 calls per hour, the chance of exactly 2 calls in an hour is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{3^2e^{-3}}{2!} = 4.5e^{-3}\\approx0.224</span>, and the chance of none is <span class=\"m\">e^{-3}\\approx0.050</span>.</p>\n<p>Where does this formula come from? Chop the hour into <span class=\"m\">n</span> tiny slots, each with a small chance <span class=\"m\">p = \\lambda/n</span> of containing a call. The count is <span class=\"m\">\\text{Bin}(n, \\lambda/n)</span>, and as <span class=\"m\">n\\to\\infty</span> the binomial PMF converges to the Poisson PMF. So the Poisson is the binomial for \"many trials, each very unlikely,\" with <span class=\"m\">np = \\lambda</span> held fixed. Its mean and variance are both <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>, which is a handy check on real data: if your counts have a variance much bigger than their mean, a plain Poisson model is wrong.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Poisson distribution</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Counts of events occurring independently at average rate <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>: <span class=\"m\">P(X=k) = \\lambda^ke^{-\\lambda}/k!</span>; mean and variance both equal <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-discrete-uniform-distribution\" data-h=\"3\">The discrete uniform distribution</h3>\n<p>Finally, when <span class=\"m\">X</span> is equally likely to be any of the integers <span class=\"m\">a, a+1, \\dots, b</span>, it is <strong>discrete uniform</strong>. A fair die is uniform on <span class=\"m\">1,\\dots,6</span>. With <span class=\"m\">m = b - a + 1</span> values, each has probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac1m</span>, the mean is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{a + b}{2}</span> and the variance is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{m^2 - 1}{12}</span>. For the die, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{36 - 1}{12} = \\frac{35}{12}</span>, matching what we computed directly before.</p>\n<h3 id=\"choosing-the-right-model\" data-h=\"3\">Choosing the right model</h3>\n<p>Ask what is being counted. A fixed number of independent yes/no trials: binomial. Trials until the first success: geometric. Events in a continuous stretch of time or space with a constant rate: Poisson. All values equally likely: uniform. A common mistake is using the binomial when <span class=\"m\">p</span> changes from trial to trial or trials are dependent. Another is forgetting whether a geometric variable counts the trials <em>including</em> the success (our convention, starting at 1) or the failures <em>before</em> it (starting at 0); textbooks use both, so always check.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Bernoulli is one trial with mean <span class=\"m\">p</span>. Binomial counts successes in <span class=\"m\">n</span> trials, with mean <span class=\"m\">np</span> and variance <span class=\"m\">np(1-p)</span>. Geometric waits for the first success, with mean <span class=\"m\">1/p</span>, and is memoryless. Poisson counts rare events at rate <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>, with mean and variance <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>, and is the limit of the binomial. Matching a situation to its story is the key skill.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"distribution\" data-args='{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;binomial&quot;, &quot;lo&quot;: 5, &quot;hi&quot;: 5}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Shade a single bar to read off an exact probability.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Set <span class=\"m\">n = 10</span>, <span class=\"m\">p = 0.5</span> and shade from 5 to 5. Confirm <span class=\"m\">P(X = 5)\\approx0.246</span>.\n2. Shade from 0 to 10 to see the bars sum to 1. Then raise <span class=\"m\">p</span> to 0.8 and read the new mean <span class=\"m\">np = 8</span> and variance <span class=\"m\">np(1-p) = 1.6</span>.\n3. Set <span class=\"m\">n = 40</span> and <span class=\"m\">p = 0.1</span>, note the mean 4 and variance 3.6, then switch to \"poisson\" with <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 4</span> and compare the bar shapes.\n4. Switch to \"geometric\" and set <span class=\"m\">p = 0.17</span> (close to a die). See how the bars decay and how large the variance is.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the binomial is symmetric at <span class=\"m\">p = 0.5</span> and skewed otherwise. With many trials and small <span class=\"m\">p</span>, it looks almost exactly like a Poisson with the same mean, which is the limit argument in the article. The geometric always has its tallest bar at 1, yet its mean is <span class=\"m\">1/p</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{Bernoulli}(p): \\; E = p,\\; \\operatorname{Var} = p(1-p)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Bin}(n,p): \\; P(k) = \\binom nk p^k(1-p)^{n-k},\\; E = np,\\; \\operatorname{Var} = np(1-p)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Geom}(p): \\; P(k) = (1-p)^{k-1}p,\\; E = \\tfrac1p,\\; \\operatorname{Var} = \\tfrac{1-p}{p^2}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Poisson}(\\lambda): \\; P(k) = \\frac{\\lambda^ke^{-\\lambda}}{k!},\\; E = \\operatorname{Var} = \\lambda</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Uniform}\\{a..b\\}: \\; E = \\tfrac{a+b}2,\\; \\operatorname{Var} = \\tfrac{m^2-1}{12},\\; m = b-a+1</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Poisson limit of the binomial</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">n\\to\\infty</span> and <span class=\"m\">p = \\lambda/n</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\binom nk p^k(1-p)^{n-k}\\to\\dfrac{\\lambda^ke^{-\\lambda}}{k!}</span> for each fixed <span class=\"m\">k</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Write <span class=\"m\">\\binom nk\\frac{\\lambda^k}{n^k}(1 - \\frac\\lambda n)^{n-k} = \\frac{\\lambda^k}{k!}\\cdot\\frac{n(n-1)\\cdots(n-k+1)}{n^k}\\cdot(1-\\frac\\lambda n)^n\\cdot(1-\\frac\\lambda n)^{-k}</span>. As <span class=\"m\">n\\to\\infty</span> the second factor tends to 1, the third to <span class=\"m\">e^{-\\lambda}</span> and the fourth to 1.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Memorylessness of the geometric distribution</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">X\\sim\\text{Geom}(p)</span> and integers <span class=\"m\">m, k\\ge0</span>: <span class=\"m\">P(X &gt; m + k\\mid X &gt; m) = P(X &gt; k)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">X &gt; j</span> means the first <span class=\"m\">j</span> trials fail, so <span class=\"m\">P(X &gt; j) = (1-p)^j</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">P(X &gt; m+k\\mid X &gt; m) = \\frac{(1-p)^{m+k}}{(1-p)^m} = (1-p)^k</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A multiple-choice test has 5 questions with 4 options each. A student guesses. Find P(at least 4 correct).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">X\\sim\\text{Bin}(5, \\frac14)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(X = 4) = \\binom54(\\frac14)^4\\frac34 = \\frac{15}{1024}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(X = 5) = (\\frac14)^5 = \\frac{1}{1024}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(X\\ge4) = \\frac{16}{1024} = \\frac{1}{64}\\approx0.0156</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A website gets 2 sign-ups per hour on average. Find P(at least 1 sign-up in an hour).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">X\\sim\\text{Poisson}(2)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">P(X = 0) = e^{-2}\\approx0.135</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(X\\ge1) = 1 - 0.135 = 0.865</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A salesperson closes each call with probability 0.2, independently. Find the expected calls to the first sale and P(first sale on call 3).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Geometric with <span class=\"m\">p = 0.2</span>: <span class=\"m\">E[X] = 1/0.2 = 5</span> calls.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(X = 3) = 0.8^2\\times0.2 = 0.128</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A printer produces pages with typos at an average rate of 0.5 per page. (a) Find the probability a page has no typos. (b) A 4-page document is printed. Find the probability the whole document has at most one typo.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">P(0) = e^{-0.5}\\approx0.607</span>. (b) Counts over 4 pages are Poisson with <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 4\\times0.5 = 2</span>. <span class=\"m\">P(X\\le1) = e^{-2}(1 + 2) = 3e^{-2}\\approx0.406</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Bernoulli and binomial likelihoods underlie logistic regression and binary cross-entropy: the loss <span class=\"m\">-[y\\log\\hat p + (1-y)\\log(1-\\hat p)]</span> is the negative log of a Bernoulli PMF. Poisson regression models counts such as clicks or page views, and the multinomial generalizes the binomial to many categories for softmax classifiers.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The geometric distribution describes retries: if a network packet is lost with probability <span class=\"m\">q</span>, the number of transmissions until success is geometric with mean <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{1-q}</span>. Poisson processes model request arrivals in queueing theory, which is used to size servers and buffers.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Poisson counts appear everywhere: goals in a football match (about 2.7 per game), radioactive decays per second, and customers arriving at a shop. The classic first example was soldiers in the Prussian army killed by horse kicks per year, which fit a Poisson distribution closely.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">X\\sim\\text{Bin}(20, 0.3)</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">E[X]</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">np = 20\\times0.3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">X\\sim\\text{Bin}(20, 0.3)</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4.2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">np(1-p) = 20\\times0.3\\times0.7</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">X\\sim\\text{Poisson}(4)</span>. What is <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Poisson mean and variance are both <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A coin with <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{heads}) = 0.25</span> is flipped until heads. Expected number of flips?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Geometric mean <span class=\"m\">1/p</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Count the defective items among 50 items made independently, each defective with probability 0.02. Which model fits best?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Binomial(50, 0.02)</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Geometric(0.02)</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Discrete uniform</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A fixed number of independent trials with the same success probability.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Flip a fair coin 4 times. Find <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{exactly 2 heads})</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3/8|0.375\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\binom42/16 = 6/16</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "continuous-rv": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"when-values-form-a-continuum\" data-h=\"3\">When values form a continuum</h3>\n<p>How long will you wait for the next bus? How tall is a randomly chosen adult? What fraction of a battery's charge is left? These quantities don't come in a list of separate values; they can be anything in an interval, like 4.2 minutes or 4.21837 minutes. Such quantities are modelled by <strong>continuous random variables</strong>, and they force us to rethink what probability of a single value means.</p>\n<p>Here's the puzzle. Suppose a spinner points to a random number between 0 and 1, every number equally likely. What is the probability it lands on exactly 0.5? There are infinitely many possible values. If each had the same positive probability, even a tiny one, the total would be infinite rather than 1. So the probability of any single exact value must be <strong>zero</strong>. Yet the spinner certainly lands somewhere. The resolution is to stop assigning probability to points and start assigning it to <strong>intervals</strong>. The chance of landing between 0.2 and 0.5 is clearly 0.3, the length of that interval.</p>\n<h3 id=\"probability-density-functions\" data-h=\"3\">Probability density functions</h3>\n<p>For a general continuous random variable, we describe how probability is spread along the number line with a <strong>probability density function</strong> (PDF) <span class=\"m\">f(x)</span>. Probabilities are <strong>areas under the curve</strong>:\n<span class=\"mb\">P(a\\le X\\le b) = \\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx.</span>\nThink of <span class=\"m\">f</span> as describing where probability is concentrated, like the density of sand piled along a ruler: where the pile is tall, values are more likely. For a PDF to make sense we need <span class=\"m\">f(x)\\ge0</span> everywhere and total area 1: <span class=\"m\">\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}f(x)\\,dx = 1</span>.</p>\n<p>Importantly, <span class=\"m\">f(x)</span> itself is <strong>not</strong> a probability. It can be bigger than 1. A spinner on <span class=\"m\">[0, \\frac12]</span> has density <span class=\"m\">f(x) = 2</span> there, because the total area <span class=\"m\">2\\times\\frac12</span> must equal 1. What <span class=\"m\">f(x)</span> tells you is probability per unit length: for a small interval of width <span class=\"m\">\\Delta x</span> around <span class=\"m\">x</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(x\\le X\\le x + \\Delta x)\\approx f(x)\\Delta x</span>.</p>\n<p>Because single points have zero probability, it doesn't matter whether interval endpoints are included: <span class=\"m\">P(a\\le X\\le b) = P(a &lt; X &lt; b)</span> for continuous variables. That is not true for discrete ones.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Probability density function (PDF)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A function <span class=\"m\">f(x)\\ge0</span> with total area 1 such that <span class=\"m\">P(a\\le X\\le b) = \\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx</span>. Its height is probability per unit length, not a probability.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-cdf-ties-it-together\" data-h=\"3\">The CDF ties it together</h3>\n<p>The cumulative distribution function <span class=\"m\">F(x) = P(X\\le x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(t)\\,dt</span> works exactly as for discrete variables, except now it is a smooth curve instead of a staircase. By the fundamental theorem of calculus, the density is the derivative of the CDF: <span class=\"m\">f(x) = F'(x)</span>. And interval probabilities are differences: <span class=\"m\">P(a\\le X\\le b) = F(b) - F(a)</span>. In practice we often use the CDF to avoid integrating again and again.</p>\n<p>The CDF also gives <strong>percentiles</strong> (quantiles). The median <span class=\"m\">m</span> solves <span class=\"m\">F(m) = 0.5</span>, and the 90th percentile solves <span class=\"m\">F(x) = 0.9</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-continuous-uniform-distribution\" data-h=\"3\">The continuous uniform distribution</h3>\n<p>The spinner is the <strong>uniform distribution</strong> on <span class=\"m\">[a, b]</span>: <span class=\"m\">f(x) = \\frac{1}{b - a}</span> for <span class=\"m\">a\\le x\\le b</span> and 0 elsewhere. Probabilities are just proportional to lengths. If a train arrives uniformly between 0 and 10 minutes from now, <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{wait}\\le3) = \\frac{3}{10}</span>. The CDF is <span class=\"m\">F(x) = \\frac{x - a}{b - a}</span> on the interval.</p>\n<h3 id=\"expectation-and-variance-by-integration\" data-h=\"3\">Expectation and variance by integration</h3>\n<p>Everything you learned for discrete variables carries over, with sums replaced by integrals and PMFs by densities:\n<span class=\"mb\">E[X] = \\int x f(x)\\,dx,\\qquad E[g(X)] = \\int g(x)f(x)\\,dx,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(X) = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2.</span>\nAll the rules from the previous lesson, linearity, <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(aX + b) = a^2\\operatorname{Var}(X)</span> and so on, still apply.</p>\n<p>For the uniform on <span class=\"m\">[0, 1]</span>: <span class=\"m\">E[X] = \\int_0^1 x\\,dx = \\frac12</span>, and <span class=\"m\">E[X^2] = \\int_0^1 x^2\\,dx = \\frac13</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X) = \\frac13 - \\frac14 = \\frac{1}{12}</span>. For a general uniform on <span class=\"m\">[a, b]</span>, the mean is the midpoint <span class=\"m\">\\frac{a + b}{2}</span> and the variance is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{(b - a)^2}{12}</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-exponential-distribution-waiting-times\" data-h=\"3\">The exponential distribution: waiting times</h3>\n<p>Calls arrive at a help desk at an average rate of <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> per minute, independently (a Poisson process, from the last lesson). How long until the next call? This waiting time <span class=\"m\">T</span> has the <strong>exponential distribution</strong>:\n<span class=\"mb\">f(t) = \\lambda e^{-\\lambda t},\\quad t\\ge0.</span>\nThe CDF comes from integrating: <span class=\"m\">F(t) = 1 - e^{-\\lambda t}</span>. This has a lovely interpretation: <span class=\"m\">P(T &gt; t) = e^{-\\lambda t}</span> is exactly the Poisson probability of <em>zero</em> calls in time <span class=\"m\">t</span>, since that count has mean <span class=\"m\">\\lambda t</span>. If <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 2</span> calls per minute, the chance of waiting more than 1 minute is <span class=\"m\">e^{-2}\\approx0.135</span>.</p>\n<p>The mean waiting time is <span class=\"m\">E[T] = \\frac1\\lambda</span> (half a minute here), and the variance is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{\\lambda^2}</span>. The median is smaller than the mean: solving <span class=\"m\">1 - e^{-\\lambda m} = \\frac12</span> gives <span class=\"m\">m = \\frac{\\ln2}{\\lambda}\\approx\\frac{0.693}{\\lambda}</span>. The long right tail of occasional long waits pulls the mean up.</p>\n<p>Like the geometric distribution, the exponential is <strong>memoryless</strong>: <span class=\"m\">P(T &gt; s + t\\mid T &gt; s) = P(T &gt; t)</span>. Having already waited 5 minutes doesn't make the call any more \"due.\" The exponential is the only continuous distribution with this property.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exponential distribution</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Waiting time until the next event in a process with rate <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>: <span class=\"m\">f(t) = \\lambda e^{-\\lambda t}</span> for <span class=\"m\">t\\ge0</span>, CDF <span class=\"m\">1 - e^{-\\lambda t}</span>, mean <span class=\"m\">1/\\lambda</span>, variance <span class=\"m\">1/\\lambda^2</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"medians-and-percentiles-of-a-continuous-variable\" data-h=\"3\">Medians and percentiles of a continuous variable</h3>\n<p>The CDF also answers questions in reverse. The <strong>median</strong> of a continuous random variable is the value <span class=\"m\">m</span> with <span class=\"m\">F(m) = 0.5</span>: half the probability lies on each side. More generally the <span class=\"m\">q</span>-th quantile solves <span class=\"m\">F(x) = q</span>. For an exponential waiting time with rate <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>, <span class=\"m\">F(x) = 1 - e^{-\\lambda x}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">1 - e^{-\\lambda m} = 0.5</span> gives <span class=\"m\">m = \\frac{\\ln 2}{\\lambda}</span>. If buses arrive at rate <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 0.5</span> per minute, the mean wait is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{\\lambda} = 2</span> minutes but the median is only <span class=\"m\">\\frac{0.693}{0.5}\\approx1.39</span> minutes. Half of all waits are shorter than about 1.4 minutes, while a few long waits pull the mean up. This gap between mean and median is the signature of a right-skewed distribution, and it's why the median is often the better summary of a \"typical\" wait, income or file size.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Reading <span class=\"m\">f(x)</span> as a probability is the classic error; only areas are probabilities. Forgetting that a density must integrate to 1 is another: if a problem gives <span class=\"m\">f(x) = cx^2</span> on <span class=\"m\">[0, 1]</span>, you must solve <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^1 cx^2\\,dx = \\frac c3 = 1</span> to get <span class=\"m\">c = 3</span>. Mixing up rate and mean in the exponential is common too: rate 2 per minute means mean wait <span class=\"m\">\\frac12</span> minute, not 2 minutes. Finally, remember that <span class=\"m\">P(X = a) = 0</span> for continuous <span class=\"m\">X</span> does not mean the value <span class=\"m\">a</span> is impossible, only that it has no probability mass by itself.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Continuous random variables take values in intervals, each single value having probability zero. A density <span class=\"m\">f(x)\\ge0</span> with total area 1 gives probabilities as areas, <span class=\"m\">P(a\\le X\\le b) = \\int_a^b f</span>, and <span class=\"m\">f = F'</span>. Means and variances are integrals. The uniform spreads probability evenly; the exponential models memoryless waiting times with mean <span class=\"m\">1/\\lambda</span>.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"distribution\" data-args='{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;exponential&quot;, &quot;lo&quot;: 0, &quot;hi&quot;: 1}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The pink area is the probability. With rate 1, P(0 ≤ T ≤ 1) = 1 − e^(−1) ≈ 0.632.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 1</span>, confirm the shaded area from 0 to 1 is about 0.632, which is <span class=\"m\">1 - e^{-1}</span>.\n2. Set <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 2</span> and shade from 1 to 8. The area <span class=\"m\">\\approx e^{-2}\\approx0.135</span> is the chance of waiting more than a minute at 2 calls per minute.\n3. Raise <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> to 4 and look at the height of the curve at 0: it is 4, bigger than 1, yet the total area is still 1.\n4. Switch to \"uniform\" with <span class=\"m\">a = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = 6</span> and shade from 2 to 5: the area is width times height, <span class=\"m\">3\\times\\frac16 = 0.5</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> probability lives in the pink area, never in the height. Increasing <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> squeezes the curve toward 0 and makes it taller, and the dashed mean <span class=\"m\">1/\\lambda</span> moves left. For the uniform, the area is just width times height.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">P(a\\le X\\le b) = \\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx = F(b) - F(a)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f(x)\\ge0,\\quad \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x)\\,dx = 1,\\quad f(x) = F'(x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">E[X] = \\int xf(x)\\,dx,\\quad \\operatorname{Var}(X) = \\int x^2f(x)\\,dx - \\mu^2</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Uniform}[a,b]: \\; f = \\tfrac{1}{b-a},\\; E = \\tfrac{a+b}{2},\\; \\operatorname{Var} = \\tfrac{(b-a)^2}{12}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Exp}(\\lambda): \\; f = \\lambda e^{-\\lambda t},\\; F = 1 - e^{-\\lambda t},\\; E = \\tfrac1\\lambda,\\; \\operatorname{Var} = \\tfrac{1}{\\lambda^2}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Memorylessness of the exponential distribution</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">T\\sim\\text{Exp}(\\lambda)</span>, then for <span class=\"m\">s, t\\ge0</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(T &gt; s + t\\mid T &gt; s) = P(T &gt; t)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">P(T &gt; u) = 1 - F(u) = e^{-\\lambda u}</span>. So <span class=\"m\">P(T &gt; s+t\\mid T &gt; s) = \\frac{e^{-\\lambda(s+t)}}{e^{-\\lambda s}} = e^{-\\lambda t} = P(T &gt; t)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Mean of the exponential distribution</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">T\\sim\\text{Exp}(\\lambda)</span> then <span class=\"m\">E[T] = 1/\\lambda</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Integrate by parts with <span class=\"m\">u = t</span>, <span class=\"m\">dv = \\lambda e^{-\\lambda t}dt</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^\\infty t\\lambda e^{-\\lambda t}dt = [-te^{-\\lambda t}]_0^\\infty + \\int_0^\\infty e^{-\\lambda t}dt = 0 + \\frac1\\lambda</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">f(x) = cx on [0, 2] and 0 elsewhere. Find c, P(X ≤ 1) and E[X].</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\int_0^2 cx\\,dx = 2c = 1</span>, so <span class=\"m\">c = \\frac12</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(X\\le1) = \\int_0^1\\frac x2dx = \\frac14</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">E[X] = \\int_0^2 x\\cdot\\frac x2dx = \\frac{x^3}{6}\\Big|_0^2 = \\frac86 = \\frac43</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A light bulb's lifetime is exponential with mean 1000 hours. Find P(it lasts more than 1500 hours) and its median lifetime.</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\lambda = \\frac{1}{1000}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(T &gt; 1500) = e^{-1.5}\\approx0.223</span>.</li>\n<li>Median <span class=\"m\">= 1000\\ln2\\approx693</span> hours.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">X is uniform on [2, 8]. Find P(3 &lt; X &lt; 6), E[X] and Var(X).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Density <span class=\"m\">\\frac16</span>; <span class=\"m\">P = \\frac{6 - 3}{6} = \\frac12</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">E[X] = 5</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X) = \\frac{36}{12} = 3</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The time (in minutes) a customer spends in a shop has density <span class=\"m\">f(t) = \\frac{t}{50}</span> for <span class=\"m\">0\\le t\\le10</span> and 0 otherwise. (a) Check that <span class=\"m\">f</span> is a valid density. (b) Find the CDF and the median time. (c) Find the mean time.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">f\\ge0</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^{10}\\frac{t}{50}dt = \\frac{100}{100} = 1</span>. (b) <span class=\"m\">F(t) = \\frac{t^2}{100}</span> on <span class=\"m\">[0, 10]</span>; the median solves <span class=\"m\">\\frac{m^2}{100} = \\frac12</span>, so <span class=\"m\">m = \\sqrt{50}\\approx7.07</span> minutes. (c) <span class=\"m\">E[T] = \\int_0^{10}\\frac{t^2}{50}dt = \\frac{1000}{150} = \\frac{20}{3}\\approx6.67</span> minutes.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Generative models such as normalizing flows and diffusion models learn densities over continuous data like images or audio. Continuous densities appear in likelihoods for regression, in weight initialization (uniform or normal), and in dropout-like noise. Sampling from any distribution can be done by the inverse CDF method: if <span class=\"m\">U</span> is uniform on <span class=\"m\">[0,1]</span>, then <span class=\"m\">F^{-1}(U)</span> has CDF <span class=\"m\">F</span>; for the exponential, <span class=\"m\">-\\ln(1-U)/\\lambda</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Simulations and load tests generate exponential inter-arrival times to mimic real traffic, and random number libraries build every continuous distribution from a uniform generator. Reliability engineers model component lifetimes with exponential distributions when the failure rate is constant.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Radioactive decay follows the exponential law: the half-life is the median, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\ln2}{\\lambda}</span>. Carbon-14 has a half-life of about 5730 years, which is how archaeologists date organic material.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">X</span> is uniform on <span class=\"m\">[0, 4]</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">P(1\\le X\\le2)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/4|0.25\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Length 1 out of 4.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For a continuous random variable, what is <span class=\"m\">P(X = 3)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">0</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">f(3)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">It depends on the distribution</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Single points have zero area under the density.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">f(x) = 3x^2</span> on <span class=\"m\">[0, 1]</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">P(X\\le\\frac12)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/8|0.125\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\int_0^{1/2}3x^2dx = (1/2)^3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">T\\sim\\text{Exp}(\\lambda = 0.5)</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">E[T]</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Mean is <span class=\"m\">1/\\lambda</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Can a density function take a value larger than 1?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Uniform on <span class=\"m\">[0, 0.25]</span> has density 4; only the total area must be 1.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Uniform on <span class=\"m\">[0, 6]</span>: find the variance.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(6-0)^2/12 = 3</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "normal-distribution": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-most-important-curve-in-statistics\" data-h=\"3\">The most important curve in statistics</h3>\n<p>Measure the heights of a thousand adults, the errors of a thousand repeated lab measurements, or the average scores of a thousand classes, and draw a histogram. Again and again you get the same shape: a symmetric hump in the middle with tails that fade quickly on both sides. This is the <strong>normal distribution</strong>, also called the Gaussian distribution or the bell curve. It is the default model for measurement noise, the shape that averages converge to (the central limit theorem, later in this course), and the starting point for most of classical statistics and a great deal of machine learning.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-formula-and-its-two-parameters\" data-h=\"3\">The formula and its two parameters</h3>\n<p>A normal random variable <span class=\"m\">X</span> with mean <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span> and standard deviation <span class=\"m\">\\sigma</span>, written <span class=\"m\">X\\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)</span>, has density\n<span class=\"mb\">f(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\\exp\\!\\Big(-\\frac{(x - \\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\Big).</span>\nIt looks intimidating, but each piece has a job. The squared distance <span class=\"m\">(x - \\mu)^2</span> is smallest at <span class=\"m\">x = \\mu</span>, so the curve peaks at the mean and is symmetric around it. Dividing by <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2</span> measures that distance in units of the standard deviation. The exponential of a negative number makes the curve fall off extremely fast as you move away: values 4 standard deviations from the mean are rarer than 1 in 15,000. The constant in front, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}}</span>, is exactly what makes the total area equal 1.</p>\n<p>The two parameters control location and spread. Changing <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span> slides the curve left or right without changing its shape. Changing <span class=\"m\">\\sigma</span> stretches it: a larger <span class=\"m\">\\sigma</span> makes it wider and flatter, a smaller one makes it narrow and tall. The mean, median and mode are all equal to <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span>, and the inflection points, where the curve changes from bending down to bending up, are at <span class=\"m\">\\mu\\pm\\sigma</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Normal distribution</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)</span> has density <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-(x-\\mu)^2/(2\\sigma^2)}</span>, a symmetric bell curve with mean <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span> and standard deviation <span class=\"m\">\\sigma</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-standard-normal-and-z-scores\" data-h=\"3\">The standard normal and z-scores</h3>\n<p>There is no simple formula for the area under a bell curve, so we need tables or software. Fortunately, one table serves every normal distribution, because all normal curves are the same shape after rescaling. The <strong>standard normal</strong> <span class=\"m\">Z\\sim N(0, 1)</span> has mean 0 and standard deviation 1, and its CDF is written <span class=\"m\">\\Phi(z) = P(Z\\le z)</span>.</p>\n<p>To use it for any normal variable, <strong>standardize</strong>: compute the <strong>z-score</strong>\n<span class=\"mb\">z = \\frac{x - \\mu}{\\sigma},</span>\nwhich says how many standard deviations <span class=\"m\">x</span> is above the mean. If <span class=\"m\">X\\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)</span>, then <span class=\"m\">Z = \\frac{X - \\mu}{\\sigma}\\sim N(0, 1)</span>, which follows from the rules for shifting and scaling. So <span class=\"m\">P(X\\le x) = \\Phi\\big(\\frac{x - \\mu}{\\sigma}\\big)</span>.</p>\n<p>Suppose adult heights are <span class=\"m\">N(170, 10^2)</span> in centimetres. What fraction of people are taller than 185 cm? The z-score is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{185 - 170}{10} = 1.5</span>. From a table, <span class=\"m\">\\Phi(1.5)\\approx0.9332</span>, so <span class=\"m\">P(X &gt; 185) = 1 - 0.9332 = 0.0668</span>, about 6.7%.</p>\n<p>Going the other way, what height is the 90th percentile? We need <span class=\"m\">z</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\Phi(z) = 0.9</span>, which is <span class=\"m\">z\\approx1.2816</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">x = \\mu + z\\sigma = 170 + 1.2816\\times10\\approx182.8</span> cm.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Z-score</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">z = (x - \\mu)/\\sigma</span>: the number of standard deviations a value lies above (positive) or below (negative) the mean.</p></div></div>\n<p>A few values of <span class=\"m\">\\Phi</span> are worth remembering: <span class=\"m\">\\Phi(0) = 0.5</span> by symmetry, <span class=\"m\">\\Phi(1)\\approx0.8413</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\Phi(1.645)\\approx0.95</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\Phi(1.96)\\approx0.975</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\Phi(2.576)\\approx0.995</span>. Symmetry also gives <span class=\"m\">\\Phi(-z) = 1 - \\Phi(z)</span>, so tables only need positive <span class=\"m\">z</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-68-95-99-7-rule\" data-h=\"3\">The 68-95-99.7 rule</h3>\n<p>For quick estimates, remember that about <strong>68%</strong> of a normal distribution lies within one standard deviation of the mean, about <strong>95%</strong> within two, and about <strong>99.7%</strong> within three. With heights <span class=\"m\">N(170, 10^2)</span>, roughly 68% of people are between 160 and 180 cm, and 95% are between 150 and 190 cm. Someone 200 cm tall is 3 standard deviations above the mean, a height reached by only about 0.15% of people. Precisely, <span class=\"m\">P(|Z|\\le1) = 2\\Phi(1) - 1\\approx0.6827</span>, and <span class=\"m\">P(|Z|\\le1.96) = 0.95</span> exactly, which is where the famous 1.96 in confidence intervals comes from.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sums-of-normal-variables\" data-h=\"3\">Sums of normal variables</h3>\n<p>Normals have a special closure property: a linear function of a normal is normal, and the sum of <em>independent</em> normals is normal. Means add and variances add:\n<span class=\"mb\">X\\sim N(\\mu_1, \\sigma_1^2),\\ Y\\sim N(\\mu_2, \\sigma_2^2)\\text{ independent}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ X + Y\\sim N(\\mu_1 + \\mu_2,\\ \\sigma_1^2 + \\sigma_2^2).</span>\nIf a commute has a walking part <span class=\"m\">N(10, 2^2)</span> minutes and a train part <span class=\"m\">N(25, 3^2)</span> minutes, independent, the total is <span class=\"m\">N(35, 13)</span> with standard deviation <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{13}\\approx3.61</span> minutes. Note that it's the variances, not the standard deviations, that add. In particular the average of <span class=\"m\">n</span> independent <span class=\"m\">N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)</span> values is exactly <span class=\"m\">N(\\mu, \\sigma^2/n)</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"approximating-the-binomial\" data-h=\"3\">Approximating the binomial</h3>\n<p>When <span class=\"m\">n</span> is large, a binomial <span class=\"m\">\\text{Bin}(n, p)</span> looks very close to a normal with the same mean <span class=\"m\">np</span> and variance <span class=\"m\">np(1-p)</span>. A common rule of thumb is that the approximation is good when <span class=\"m\">np\\ge10</span> and <span class=\"m\">n(1-p)\\ge10</span>. Because the binomial is discrete, we use a <strong>continuity correction</strong>: treat the integer <span class=\"m\">k</span> as the interval <span class=\"m\">[k - 0.5, k + 0.5]</span>. For 100 fair coin flips, <span class=\"m\">\\mu = 50</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 5</span>. The chance of 60 or more heads is about <span class=\"m\">P(Z\\ge\\frac{59.5 - 50}{5}) = P(Z\\ge1.9) = 1 - 0.9713 = 0.0287</span>; the exact binomial answer is 0.0284.</p>\n<h3 id=\"comparing-values-from-different-normal-distributions\" data-h=\"3\">Comparing values from different normal distributions</h3>\n<p>Standardizing also lets us compare results measured on different scales. Suppose Maya scores 82 on a chemistry exam where scores are <span class=\"m\">N(70, 8^2)</span>, and 88 on a history exam where scores are <span class=\"m\">N(80, 5^2)</span>. The history score is higher in raw points, but her chemistry <span class=\"m\">z</span>-score is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{82 - 70}{8} = 1.5</span> while her history <span class=\"m\">z</span>-score is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{88 - 80}{5} = 1.6</span>. Relative to classmates, the history result is slightly more impressive: it beats about 94.5% of students, against 93.3% for chemistry. Because every normal distribution becomes the same standard normal after standardizing, a <span class=\"m\">z</span>-score means the same thing whatever the original units, which is why test publishers, growth charts for children and quality engineers all report results as <span class=\"m\">z</span>-scores or the percentiles that go with them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The most frequent error is using the variance instead of the standard deviation when standardizing: in <span class=\"m\">N(170, 100)</span> the second number is <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2 = 100</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 10</span>. Another is forgetting the direction: tables give <span class=\"m\">P(Z\\le z)</span>, so \"greater than\" needs <span class=\"m\">1 - \\Phi(z)</span>. Don't add standard deviations when summing independent normals. And don't assume every bell-shaped looking dataset is normal: incomes, file sizes and waiting times are often skewed with heavy right tails, and treating them as normal badly underestimates extreme values.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The normal <span class=\"m\">N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)</span> is a symmetric bell curve centred at <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span> with spread <span class=\"m\">\\sigma</span>. Standardize with <span class=\"m\">z = (x - \\mu)/\\sigma</span> and use <span class=\"m\">\\Phi</span> to find probabilities and percentiles. About 68%, 95% and 99.7% of values lie within 1, 2 and 3 standard deviations. Independent normals add to a normal with added means and variances, and large binomials are approximately normal.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"distribution\" data-args='{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;normal&quot;, &quot;lo&quot;: -1, &quot;hi&quot;: 1}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The pink area between −1 and 1 is about 0.683: the 68 in the 68-95-99.7 rule.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With <span class=\"m\">\\mu = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 1</span>, shade from −1 to 1, then from −2 to 2, then from −3 to 3. Read off roughly 0.683, 0.954 and 0.997.\n2. Shade from −1.96 to 1.96 and confirm the area is 0.95.\n3. Shade from 1.5 to 8 to get <span class=\"m\">P(Z &gt; 1.5)\\approx0.067</span>, the tall-people answer from the article.\n4. Change <span class=\"m\">\\sigma</span> to 2 and keep the shading from −1 to 1. The area drops to about 0.383, because now ±1 is only half a standard deviation.\n5. Move <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span> and watch the whole curve slide while its shape stays fixed.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> only the distance measured in standard deviations matters. Any normal probability can be turned into a standard normal one by standardizing, which is why one table is enough.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">f(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-\\frac{(x-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">Z = \\frac{X - \\mu}{\\sigma}\\sim N(0,1),\\qquad P(X\\le x) = \\Phi\\!\\Big(\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\Big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\Phi(-z) = 1 - \\Phi(z)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(|Z|\\le1)\\approx0.683,\\ P(|Z|\\le2)\\approx0.954,\\ P(|Z|\\le3)\\approx0.997</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">aX + b\\sim N(a\\mu + b, a^2\\sigma^2)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">X + Y\\sim N(\\mu_1+\\mu_2, \\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2)\\ \\text{(independent)}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Standardization</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">X\\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)</span> then <span class=\"m\">Z = (X - \\mu)/\\sigma\\sim N(0, 1)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">P(Z\\le z) = P(X\\le\\mu + \\sigma z) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\mu+\\sigma z}\\frac{1}{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-(x-\\mu)^2/2\\sigma^2}dx</span>. Substitute <span class=\"m\">u = (x - \\mu)/\\sigma</span>, <span class=\"m\">dx = \\sigma\\,du</span>: this becomes <span class=\"m\">\\int_{-\\infty}^z\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-u^2/2}du = \\Phi(z)</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam scores are N(70, 8²). What fraction score between 62 and 86?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">z</span>-scores: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{62 - 70}{8} = -1</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{86 - 70}{8} = 2</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(-1\\le Z\\le2) = \\Phi(2) - \\Phi(-1) = 0.9772 - 0.1587 = 0.8185</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Bags of flour weigh N(1000, 15²) grams. What weight is exceeded by only 5% of bags?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>We need <span class=\"m\">\\Phi(z) = 0.95</span>, so <span class=\"m\">z = 1.645</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">x = 1000 + 1.645\\times15\\approx1024.7</span> grams.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Two independent measurements are N(5, 1) and N(3, 4). Find P(X − Y &gt; 0).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">X - Y\\sim N(5 - 3, 1 + 4) = N(2, 5)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(X - Y &gt; 0) = P(Z &gt; \\frac{0 - 2}{\\sqrt5}) = P(Z &gt; -0.894) = \\Phi(0.894)\\approx0.814</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A machine fills bottles with volumes <span class=\"m\">N(500, 4^2)</span> ml. (a) What fraction of bottles contain less than 494 ml? (b) A pack holds 4 independent bottles. What is the probability the pack's total volume is less than 1990 ml?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">z = \\frac{494 - 500}{4} = -1.5</span>, so <span class=\"m\">P = \\Phi(-1.5) = 1 - 0.9332 = 0.0668</span>. (b) The total is <span class=\"m\">N(2000, 4\\times16) = N(2000, 64)</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 8</span>. <span class=\"m\">z = \\frac{1990 - 2000}{8} = -1.25</span>, so <span class=\"m\">P = \\Phi(-1.25) = 1 - 0.8944 = 0.1056</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Least-squares regression is exactly maximum likelihood under Gaussian noise: maximizing <span class=\"m\">\\prod e^{-(y_i - \\hat y_i)^2/2\\sigma^2}</span> means minimizing <span class=\"m\">\\sum(y_i - \\hat y_i)^2</span>. Neural network weights are often initialized from normal distributions with carefully chosen variances (Xavier and He initialization), batch normalization standardizes activations with z-scores, and variational autoencoders and diffusion models are built on Gaussian noise.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Anomaly detection systems flag measurements with <span class=\"m\">|z| &gt; 3</span> as unusual, such as a server whose response time is 3 standard deviations above normal. The Box-Muller transform generates normal random numbers from two uniform ones.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Standardized tests are scaled so scores are roughly normal: IQ tests use <span class=\"m\">N(100, 15^2)</span>, so a score of 130 is 2 standard deviations above the mean, the top 2.3%. Manufacturers use \"six sigma\" quality targets to keep defects far out in the tails.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">X\\sim N(50, 10^2)</span>. What is the z-score of 65?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1.5|3/2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(65 - 50)/10</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">About what fraction of a normal distribution lies within 2 standard deviations of the mean?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">68%</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">95%</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">99.7%</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The 68-95-99.7 rule.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">Z\\sim N(0, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\Phi(1) = 0.8413</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">P(Z &gt; 1)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.1587\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">1 - 0.8413</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Using symmetry, find <span class=\"m\">P(Z &lt; -1)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.1587\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\Phi(-1) = 1 - \\Phi(1)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">X\\sim N(2, 9)</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y\\sim N(3, 16)</span> are independent. What is the standard deviation of <span class=\"m\">X + Y</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Variances add: <span class=\"m\">9 + 16 = 25</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the median of <span class=\"m\">N(7, 4)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The normal is symmetric, so the median equals the mean.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "joint": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-look-at-two-variables-together\" data-h=\"3\">Why look at two variables together</h3>\n<p>Real questions rarely involve one quantity in isolation. Does studying longer go with higher exam scores? Are taller people heavier? Does a customer who buys bread also buy butter? To answer these we need to describe two (or more) random variables <em>together</em>, including how they relate. This is the job of a <strong>joint distribution</strong>. It is also the foundation of machine learning, where every data point is a vector of features <span class=\"m\">x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_d</span> together with a label, and learning means understanding how those quantities depend on each other.</p>\n<h3 id=\"joint-probability-tables\" data-h=\"3\">Joint probability tables</h3>\n<p>For two discrete random variables <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span>, the <strong>joint PMF</strong> gives the probability of every pair of values: <span class=\"m\">p(x, y) = P(X = x, Y = y)</span>. Suppose a shop records whether a customer buys coffee (<span class=\"m\">X = 1</span>) or not (<span class=\"m\">X = 0</span>), and whether they buy a pastry (<span class=\"m\">Y = 1</span>) or not (<span class=\"m\">Y = 0</span>):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th><span class=\"m\">Y = 0</span></th>\n<th><span class=\"m\">Y = 1</span></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"m\">X = 0</span></td>\n<td>0.3</td>\n<td>0.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"m\">X = 1</span></td>\n<td>0.2</td>\n<td>0.4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>All four entries are non-negative and add to 1. Each one is the probability of a combination: the chance a customer buys coffee and a pastry is 0.4.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Joint distribution</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For discrete <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span>, the function <span class=\"m\">p(x, y) = P(X = x, Y = y)</span>, which is non-negative and sums to 1 over all pairs. For continuous variables, a joint density <span class=\"m\">f(x, y)</span> whose volume over a region gives its probability.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"marginal-distributions\" data-h=\"3\">Marginal distributions</h3>\n<p>From the joint table we can recover the distribution of each variable on its own, called its <strong>marginal distribution</strong>, by adding across rows or down columns. Adding across the rows gives <span class=\"m\">P(X = 0) = 0.3 + 0.1 = 0.4</span> and <span class=\"m\">P(X = 1) = 0.2 + 0.4 = 0.6</span>: 60% of customers buy coffee. Adding down the columns gives <span class=\"m\">P(Y = 0) = 0.5</span> and <span class=\"m\">P(Y = 1) = 0.5</span>. In symbols, <span class=\"m\">p_X(x) = \\sum_y p(x, y)</span>. The name comes from writing these totals in the margins of the table. Note that the marginals alone do not determine the joint table: many different tables have the same row and column totals. The joint distribution contains extra information about how the variables are connected.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Marginal distribution</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The distribution of one variable obtained from a joint distribution by summing (or integrating) out the others: <span class=\"m\">p_X(x) = \\sum_y p(x, y)</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"conditional-distributions\" data-h=\"3\">Conditional distributions</h3>\n<p>If we know a customer bought coffee, what's the chance they also bought a pastry? Restrict to the <span class=\"m\">X = 1</span> row and rescale it to sum to 1: <span class=\"m\">P(Y = 1\\mid X = 1) = \\frac{0.4}{0.6} = \\frac23</span>. For non-coffee customers, <span class=\"m\">P(Y = 1\\mid X = 0) = \\frac{0.1}{0.4} = \\frac14</span>. This is the <strong>conditional distribution</strong> of <span class=\"m\">Y</span> given <span class=\"m\">X</span>: <span class=\"m\">p(y\\mid x) = \\frac{p(x, y)}{p_X(x)}</span>. Coffee buyers are much more likely to buy pastries, so the two variables are related.</p>\n<h3 id=\"independence-of-random-variables\" data-h=\"3\">Independence of random variables</h3>\n<p><span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> are <strong>independent</strong> if knowing one tells us nothing about the other, which means the joint probabilities factor into the marginals for every pair: <span class=\"m\">p(x, y) = p_X(x)p_Y(y)</span>. Here <span class=\"m\">p_X(1)p_Y(1) = 0.6\\times0.5 = 0.3</span>, but <span class=\"m\">p(1, 1) = 0.4</span>, so coffee and pastries are not independent. In an independent table, every row would be a multiple of the same pattern. One failing cell is enough to show dependence, but to prove independence every cell must factor.</p>\n<h3 id=\"covariance-do-they-move-together\" data-h=\"3\">Covariance: do they move together?</h3>\n<p>To summarize the relationship in one number, we use the <strong>covariance</strong> from the expectation lesson, <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Cov}(X, Y) = E[XY] - E[X]E[Y]</span>. For the shop, <span class=\"m\">E[X] = 0.6</span> and <span class=\"m\">E[Y] = 0.5</span>. The product <span class=\"m\">XY</span> is 1 only when both purchases happen, so <span class=\"m\">E[XY] = 0.4</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Cov}(X, Y) = 0.4 - 0.3 = 0.1</span>. Positive covariance means the variables tend to be high together and low together.</p>\n<p>The problem with covariance is its units. If <span class=\"m\">X</span> is height in centimetres and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> is weight in kilograms, the covariance is in centimetre-kilograms, and switching to metres would divide it by 100 without changing the relationship at all. So we standardize.</p>\n<h3 id=\"correlation-a-unit-free-measure\" data-h=\"3\">Correlation: a unit-free measure</h3>\n<p>The <strong>correlation coefficient</strong> divides covariance by both standard deviations:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\rho = \\operatorname{Corr}(X, Y) = \\frac{\\operatorname{Cov}(X, Y)}{\\sigma_X\\sigma_Y}.</span>\nIt always lies between <span class=\"m\">-1</span> and <span class=\"m\">1</span>. A value of <span class=\"m\">1</span> means <span class=\"m\">Y</span> is an exact increasing linear function of <span class=\"m\">X</span>, and <span class=\"m\">-1</span> an exact decreasing one. Values near 0 mean little <em>linear</em> relationship. For the shop, <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X) = 0.6\\times0.4 = 0.24</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(Y) = 0.25</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\rho = \\frac{0.1}{\\sqrt{0.24\\times0.25}} = \\frac{0.1}{0.245}\\approx0.41</span>, a moderate positive correlation.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Correlation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\rho = \\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)/(\\sigma_X\\sigma_Y)</span>, a number in <span class=\"m\">[-1, 1]</span> measuring the strength and direction of the linear relationship between <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"zero-correlation-is-not-independence\" data-h=\"3\">Zero correlation is not independence</h3>\n<p>Independence implies zero covariance, but the reverse fails. Let <span class=\"m\">X</span> be <span class=\"m\">-1</span>, 0 or 1 with equal probability, and let <span class=\"m\">Y = X^2</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">Y</span> is completely determined by <span class=\"m\">X</span>, about as dependent as you can get. But <span class=\"m\">E[X] = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">E[XY] = E[X^3] = 0</span>, so the covariance is 0. Correlation only detects straight-line relationships; a U-shaped relationship can have zero correlation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"continuous-joint-distributions-and-the-multivariate-normal\" data-h=\"3\">Continuous joint distributions and the multivariate normal</h3>\n<p>For continuous variables, a joint density <span class=\"m\">f(x, y)</span> describes a surface over the plane, and probabilities are volumes: <span class=\"m\">P((X, Y)\\in R) = \\iint_R f(x, y)\\,dx\\,dy</span>. Marginals come from integrating out the other variable. The most important example is the <strong>bivariate normal</strong>, whose density looks like a bell-shaped hill. With means <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span> and the <strong>covariance matrix</strong>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\Sigma = \\begin{pmatrix}\\sigma_X^2 &amp; \\rho\\sigma_X\\sigma_Y\\\\ \\rho\\sigma_X\\sigma_Y &amp; \\sigma_Y^2\\end{pmatrix},</span>\nits contour lines are ellipses. When <span class=\"m\">\\rho = 0</span> the ellipses line up with the axes; as <span class=\"m\">\\rho</span> approaches <span class=\"m\">\\pm1</span> they squash into a thin diagonal cigar. In <span class=\"m\">d</span> dimensions, <span class=\"m\">N(\\mu, \\Sigma)</span> has a <span class=\"m\">d\\times d</span> covariance matrix with variances on the diagonal and covariances off it. For jointly normal variables, and only in special cases like this, zero correlation does imply independence.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Correlation does not imply causation: ice cream sales and drownings are correlated because both rise in summer. A small correlation doesn't mean no relationship, only no linear one. Don't conclude independence from the marginals alone, and don't confuse the conditional <span class=\"m\">P(Y\\mid X)</span> with the joint <span class=\"m\">P(X, Y)</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A joint distribution describes several random variables at once. Marginals come from summing out variables, and conditionals from slicing and rescaling. Variables are independent when the joint factors into marginals. Covariance measures how they move together, correlation rescales it to <span class=\"m\">[-1, 1]</span>, and the multivariate normal is described completely by a mean vector and a covariance matrix.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"scatter-corr\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> 200 points drawn from a bivariate normal with correlation ρ. The pink line is the best-fit line.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Start at <span class=\"m\">\\rho = 0.6</span>. Count roughly how many points are purple (same-sign deviations from the means) versus orange.\n2. Slide <span class=\"m\">\\rho</span> to 0 and see the cloud become round, with purple and orange points balanced.\n3. Slide to <span class=\"m\">\\rho = -0.9</span> and then to <span class=\"m\">+1</span>. Watch the cloud become a thin line and the readout <span class=\"m\">r</span> reach 1.\n4. Compare the sample correlation <span class=\"m\">r</span> in the readout with the slider <span class=\"m\">\\rho</span>. They're close but not identical, because 200 points are only a sample.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> covariance is the average product of deviations, so it's driven by whether points sit in the purple quadrants (top-right and bottom-left) or the orange ones. The cloud's elliptical shape is the contour picture of the bivariate normal described in the article.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">p_X(x) = \\sum_y p(x,y),\\qquad f_X(x) = \\int f(x,y)\\,dy</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">p(y\\mid x) = \\frac{p(x,y)}{p_X(x)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{independent}\\iff p(x,y) = p_X(x)p_Y(y)\\ \\text{for all } x,y</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y) = E[XY] - E[X]E[Y]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\rho = \\frac{\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)}{\\sigma_X\\sigma_Y},\\qquad -1\\le\\rho\\le1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{Cov}(aX+b, cY+d) = ac\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Independence implies zero covariance</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> are independent then <span class=\"m\">E[XY] = E[X]E[Y]</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Cov}(X, Y) = 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">E[XY] = \\sum_{x,y}xy\\,p_X(x)p_Y(y) = \\big(\\sum_x xp_X(x)\\big)\\big(\\sum_y yp_Y(y)\\big) = E[X]E[Y]</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Correlation is between −1 and 1</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any <span class=\"m\">X</span>, <span class=\"m\">Y</span> with positive variances, <span class=\"m\">|\\rho|\\le1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">U = (X - \\mu_X)/\\sigma_X</span> and <span class=\"m\">V = (Y - \\mu_Y)/\\sigma_Y</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(U) = \\operatorname{Var}(V) = 1</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Cov}(U, V) = \\rho</span>. Variances are non-negative: <span class=\"m\">0\\le\\operatorname{Var}(U\\pm V) = 2\\pm2\\rho</span>, which gives <span class=\"m\">-1\\le\\rho\\le1</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Using the coffee table, find P(X = 1 | Y = 1).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(Y = 1) = 0.1 + 0.4 = 0.5</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(X = 1\\mid Y = 1) = \\frac{0.4}{0.5} = 0.8</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">X and Y have joint PMF p(0,0) = 0.25, p(0,1) = 0.25, p(1,0) = 0.25, p(1,1) = 0.25. Are they independent?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Marginals: <span class=\"m\">p_X(0) = p_X(1) = 0.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">p_Y(0) = p_Y(1) = 0.5</span>.</li>\n<li>Each cell equals <span class=\"m\">0.5\\times0.5 = 0.25</span>, so yes, independent.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Var(X) = 4, Var(Y) = 9, Cov(X, Y) = 3. Find ρ and Var(X + Y).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\rho = \\frac{3}{2\\times3} = 0.5</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(X + Y) = 4 + 9 + 2(3) = 19</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> have joint PMF <span class=\"m\">p(1,1) = 0.2</span>, <span class=\"m\">p(1,2) = 0.3</span>, <span class=\"m\">p(2,1) = 0.4</span>, <span class=\"m\">p(2,2) = 0.1</span>. (a) Find the marginal distributions. (b) Find <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Cov}(X, Y)</span>. (c) Are <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> independent?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">P(X=1) = 0.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(X=2) = 0.5</span>; <span class=\"m\">P(Y=1) = 0.6</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(Y=2) = 0.4</span>. (b) <span class=\"m\">E[X] = 1.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">E[Y] = 1.4</span>, <span class=\"m\">E[XY] = 1(0.2) + 2(0.3) + 2(0.4) + 4(0.1) = 2.0</span>. <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Cov} = 2.0 - 2.1 = -0.1</span>. (c) No: <span class=\"m\">p(1,1) = 0.2\\ne0.5\\times0.6 = 0.3</span> (and the covariance is non-zero).</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every dataset is a sample from a joint distribution <span class=\"m\">p(x, y)</span>. Discriminative models learn the conditional <span class=\"m\">p(y\\mid x)</span>, while generative models learn the joint. A covariance matrix of features drives PCA, whitening and Gaussian mixture models, and highly correlated features (multicollinearity) make regression weights unstable. Gaussian processes model functions as infinite-dimensional multivariate normals.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Recommender systems and market-basket analysis estimate joint and conditional probabilities of items, like <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{butter}\\mid\\text{bread})</span>. Database query optimizers assume column independence to estimate result sizes, and they make large errors when columns are correlated.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Finance runs on covariance: portfolio risk depends on the covariance matrix of asset returns. In medicine, correlation between a risk factor and a disease is the first clue, but establishing causation needs controlled trials.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In the coffee table, what is <span class=\"m\">P(Y = 1\\mid X = 0)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/4|0.25\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">0.1/0.4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Cov}(X, Y) = 6</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_X = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_Y = 5</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">\\rho</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.6|3/5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">6/(2\\times5)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Cov}(X, Y) = 0</span>, must <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> be independent?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">Y = X^2</span> with <span class=\"m\">X</span> symmetric around 0 has zero covariance but is fully dependent.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Cov}(X, Y) = 2</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Cov}(3X, -Y + 4)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"-6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">3\\times(-1)\\times2</span>; constants don't matter.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A correlation of <span class=\"m\">-0.95</span> means...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a strong decreasing linear relationship</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">almost no relationship</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">X</span> causes <span class=\"m\">Y</span> to decrease</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Near <span class=\"m\">-1</span> means points lie close to a downward line; correlation says nothing about cause.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Joint PMF: <span class=\"m\">p(0,0) = 0.1</span>, <span class=\"m\">p(0,1) = 0.3</span>, <span class=\"m\">p(1,0) = 0.2</span>, <span class=\"m\">p(1,1) = 0.4</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">P(Y = 1)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.7|7/10\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Sum the <span class=\"m\">Y = 1</span> column: <span class=\"m\">0.3 + 0.4</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "inequalities-lln": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-averages-can-be-trusted\" data-h=\"3\">Why averages can be trusted</h3>\n<p>Flip a fair coin 10 times and you might get 7 heads, a proportion of 0.7. Flip it 10,000 times and you will almost certainly get something between 0.49 and 0.51. Casinos rely on this, pollsters rely on it, and every machine learning model trained by averaging a loss over a dataset relies on it. The <strong>law of large numbers</strong> is the theorem that makes \"the average settles down to the expected value\" precise. To prove it we need tools for bounding how likely a random variable is to be far from its mean, even when we know almost nothing about its distribution. Those tools are Markov's and Chebyshev's inequalities.</p>\n<h3 id=\"markov-s-inequality\" data-h=\"3\">Markov's inequality</h3>\n<p>Suppose the average income in a town is &#36;40,000. What fraction of people can earn &#36;200,000 or more? Without knowing anything else, we can still say: at most a fifth. If more than a fifth earned at least &#36;200,000, those people alone would push the average above <span class=\"m\">\\frac15\\times200{,}000 = 40{,}000</span>, even if everyone else earned nothing. Incomes can't be negative, so this argument is airtight.</p>\n<p>That's <strong>Markov's inequality</strong>: for any non-negative random variable <span class=\"m\">X</span> and any <span class=\"m\">a &gt; 0</span>,\n<span class=\"mb\">P(X\\ge a)\\le\\frac{E[X]}{a}.</span>\nIt is crude, but it needs only the mean and the fact that <span class=\"m\">X\\ge0</span>. With <span class=\"m\">E[X] = 40{,}000</span> and <span class=\"m\">a = 200{,}000</span>, the bound is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{40{,}000}{200{,}000} = 0.2</span>. Notice the bound is useless when <span class=\"m\">a</span> is below the mean (it would exceed 1), and it's only tight for very particular distributions. Its real value is as a building block.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Markov's inequality</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">X\\ge0</span> and <span class=\"m\">a &gt; 0</span>: <span class=\"m\">P(X\\ge a)\\le E[X]/a</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"chebyshev-s-inequality\" data-h=\"3\">Chebyshev's inequality</h3>\n<p>If we also know the variance, we can do much better, and we can bound deviations in both directions. Apply Markov's inequality to the non-negative variable <span class=\"m\">(X - \\mu)^2</span> with threshold <span class=\"m\">(k\\sigma)^2</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">P(|X - \\mu|\\ge k\\sigma) = P\\big((X - \\mu)^2\\ge k^2\\sigma^2\\big)\\le\\frac{E[(X - \\mu)^2]}{k^2\\sigma^2} = \\frac{1}{k^2}.</span>\nThis is <strong>Chebyshev's inequality</strong>: the probability of being at least <span class=\"m\">k</span> standard deviations from the mean is at most <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{k^2}</span>. For <em>any</em> distribution with a finite variance, at most <span class=\"m\">\\frac14</span> of the probability lies 2 or more standard deviations away, and at most <span class=\"m\">\\frac19</span> lies 3 or more away. Equivalently, at least 75% lies within 2 standard deviations and at least about 89% within 3.</p>\n<p>Compare this with the normal distribution, where 95% lies within 2 standard deviations. Chebyshev's 75% is weaker because it must hold for every distribution, including strange, heavy-tailed ones. When you know the distribution is normal, use the normal; when you don't, Chebyshev is a guarantee.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Chebyshev's inequality</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any <span class=\"m\">X</span> with mean <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span> and standard deviation <span class=\"m\">\\sigma</span>, and any <span class=\"m\">k &gt; 0</span>: <span class=\"m\">P(|X - \\mu|\\ge k\\sigma)\\le 1/k^2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<p>Here's an example. A factory's bolts have mean length 50 mm and standard deviation 0.2 mm, but the shape of the distribution is unknown. What fraction can be outside 49.4 to 50.6 mm? That interval is <span class=\"m\">\\pm0.6 = \\pm3\\sigma</span>, so at most <span class=\"m\">\\frac19\\approx11\\%</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-sample-mean\" data-h=\"3\">The sample mean</h3>\n<p>Now let <span class=\"m\">X_1, X_2, \\dots, X_n</span> be <strong>independent and identically distributed</strong> (i.i.d.): independent repeats of the same experiment, each with mean <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span> and variance <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2</span>. Their <strong>sample mean</strong> is <span class=\"m\">\\bar X_n = \\frac1n(X_1 + \\dots + X_n)</span>. By the rules for expectation and variance, <span class=\"m\">E[\\bar X_n] = \\mu</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X_n) = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}</span>. The average is centred on the true mean, and its spread shrinks as <span class=\"m\">n</span> grows. Ten coin flips have a proportion of heads with standard deviation <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{0.25/10}\\approx0.158</span>; ten thousand flips, <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{0.25/10000} = 0.005</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Random variables that are mutually independent and all share the same distribution, like repeated rolls of the same die.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-weak-law-of-large-numbers\" data-h=\"3\">The weak law of large numbers</h3>\n<p>Combine the two facts. Apply Chebyshev to <span class=\"m\">\\bar X_n</span>, whose variance is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}</span>. For any tolerance <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon &gt; 0</span>,\n<span class=\"mb\">P(|\\bar X_n - \\mu|\\ge\\varepsilon)\\le\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n\\varepsilon^2}.</span>\nAs <span class=\"m\">n\\to\\infty</span>, the right side goes to zero. This is the <strong>weak law of large numbers</strong>: the probability that the sample mean is off by more than any fixed amount shrinks to zero. We say <span class=\"m\">\\bar X_n</span> <strong>converges in probability</strong> to <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span>. (A stronger version, the strong law, says that with probability 1 the sequence of averages actually converges to <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span>.)</p>\n<p>The bound also tells us how many samples we need. To be at least 95% sure that a poll's proportion is within 0.05 of the truth, we need <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n(0.05)^2}\\le0.05</span>. Since a proportion has <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2 = p(1-p)\\le\\frac14</span>, it suffices that <span class=\"m\">n\\ge\\frac{0.25}{0.05\\times0.0025} = 2000</span>. The central limit theorem in the next lesson will show that about 385 is actually enough, but Chebyshev gives a guarantee with no assumptions about shape.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-the-law-does-not-say\" data-h=\"3\">What the law does not say</h3>\n<p>The law of large numbers is about <em>proportions</em> and <em>averages</em>, not about counts balancing out. After 1000 flips you might have 520 heads, an excess of 20. After a million flips the excess might be 600, which is larger, yet the proportion 0.5006 is much closer to 0.5. The deviations in the count usually grow like <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt n</span>, while the average divides by <span class=\"m\">n</span>. And it says nothing about any single flip: believing that tails is \"due\" after a run of heads is the <strong>gambler's fallacy</strong>. Independent trials have no memory. The average converges not because later flips compensate, but because early luck gets diluted by a huge number of ordinary flips.</p>\n<h3 id=\"monte-carlo-methods\" data-h=\"3\">Monte Carlo methods</h3>\n<p>The law of large numbers lets us compute expectations by simulation. To estimate <span class=\"m\">E[g(X)]</span>, draw many independent samples and average <span class=\"m\">g</span> of them. To estimate <span class=\"m\">\\pi</span>, scatter <span class=\"m\">n</span> random points in the unit square and count the fraction inside the quarter circle <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2\\le1</span>; that fraction converges to <span class=\"m\">\\frac\\pi4</span>. The error shrinks like <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{\\sqrt n}</span>, so each extra digit of accuracy costs 100 times more samples.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Markov's inequality bounds tails using only the mean of a non-negative variable. Chebyshev's inequality, <span class=\"m\">P(|X - \\mu|\\ge k\\sigma)\\le\\frac{1}{k^2}</span>, works for any distribution with finite variance. The sample mean of i.i.d. values has mean <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span> and variance <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2/n</span>, and combining these gives the law of large numbers: averages converge to the expected value. This is what justifies estimating probabilities by frequencies and expectations by simulation.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"lln\" data-args='{&quot;p&quot;: 0.3}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Five independent runs of 1000 flips. The running proportion of heads converges to the true probability.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With true <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{heads}) = 0.3</span>, press \"Flip 1000 × 5 runs\" several times. Note how wild the five curves are for the first 20 flips.\n2. Look at the curves near <span class=\"m\">n = 1000</span>. They should all be within a few hundredths of 0.3; the standard deviation there is <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{0.21/1000}\\approx0.014</span>.\n3. Change <span class=\"m\">p</span> to 0.5 and repeat. The early spread is a little wider, because <span class=\"m\">p(1-p)</span> is largest at 0.5.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> no curve \"corrects\" its early luck by producing extra tails later; the early wiggles simply get averaged away as <span class=\"m\">n</span> grows. The funnel narrows like <span class=\"m\">1/\\sqrt n</span>, which is the <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2/n</span> from the article: going from 10 to 1000 flips makes the spread 10 times smaller.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">P(X\\ge a)\\le\\frac{E[X]}{a}\\quad(X\\ge0)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(|X-\\mu|\\ge k\\sigma)\\le\\frac{1}{k^2}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">E[\\bar X_n] = \\mu,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X_n) = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(|\\bar X_n - \\mu|\\ge\\varepsilon)\\le\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n\\varepsilon^2}\\to0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Monte Carlo: } \\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n g(X_i)\\to E[g(X)]</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Markov's inequality</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">X\\ge0</span> and <span class=\"m\">a &gt; 0</span>, then <span class=\"m\">P(X\\ge a)\\le E[X]/a</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">I</span> indicate the event <span class=\"m\">X\\ge a</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">aI\\le X</span> always: when <span class=\"m\">I = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">X\\ge a</span>; when <span class=\"m\">I = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">X\\ge0</span>. Taking expectations, <span class=\"m\">aP(X\\ge a)\\le E[X]</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Weak law of large numbers</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">X_1, X_2, \\dots</span> are i.i.d. with mean <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span> and finite variance <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2</span>, then for every <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon &gt; 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(|\\bar X_n - \\mu|\\ge\\varepsilon)\\to0</span> as <span class=\"m\">n\\to\\infty</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">E[\\bar X_n] = \\mu</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X_n) = \\sigma^2/n</span>. By Chebyshev with <span class=\"m\">k\\sigma_{\\bar X} = \\varepsilon</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(|\\bar X_n - \\mu|\\ge\\varepsilon)\\le\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n\\varepsilon^2}</span>, which tends to 0.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">The average wait at a clinic is 20 minutes. Bound the probability of waiting at least an hour.</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Wait times are non-negative, so Markov applies.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(W\\ge60)\\le\\frac{20}{60} = \\frac13</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A test has mean 70 and standard deviation 5. At least what fraction of scores lie between 60 and 80?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>60 and 80 are <span class=\"m\">k = 2</span> standard deviations from the mean.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(|X - 70|\\ge10)\\le\\frac14</span>.</li>\n<li>So at least <span class=\"m\">1 - \\frac14 = \\frac34</span> of the scores lie strictly between 60 and 80.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">How many rolls of a die guarantee, by Chebyshev, P(|average − 3.5| ≥ 0.5) ≤ 0.1?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2 = \\frac{35}{12}</span>.</li>\n<li>Need <span class=\"m\">\\frac{35/12}{n(0.25)}\\le0.1</span>, so <span class=\"m\">n\\ge\\frac{35/12}{0.025} = \\frac{35}{0.3}\\approx116.7</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">n = 117</span> rolls suffice.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A website's daily visitor count has mean 1000 and standard deviation 100, with an unknown distribution. (a) Use Markov's inequality to bound <span class=\"m\">P(X\\ge2000)</span>. (b) Use Chebyshev's inequality to bound the same probability. (c) Which bound is better, and why?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">P(X\\ge2000)\\le\\frac{1000}{2000} = 0.5</span>. (b) <span class=\"m\">X\\ge2000</span> means <span class=\"m\">X - 1000\\ge1000 = 10\\sigma</span>, so <span class=\"m\">P\\le P(|X - 1000|\\ge10\\sigma)\\le\\frac{1}{100} = 0.01</span>. (c) Chebyshev is far better because it uses the variance as well as the mean.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Training minimizes an average loss over the dataset, which by the law of large numbers approximates the expected loss over the true distribution; that is the justification for empirical risk minimization. Concentration inequalities like Hoeffding's, <span class=\"m\">P(|\\bar X - \\mu|\\ge\\varepsilon)\\le2e^{-2n\\varepsilon^2}</span> for values in <span class=\"m\">[0,1]</span>, give generalization bounds and tell us how big a test set must be to trust an accuracy estimate.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Randomized algorithms use Markov's inequality to turn an expected running time into a high-probability guarantee: an algorithm with expected time <span class=\"m\">T</span> finishes within <span class=\"m\">2T</span> with probability at least <span class=\"m\">\\frac12</span>, so restarting after <span class=\"m\">2T</span> steps a few times makes failure very unlikely. Monte Carlo integration and rendering (path tracing) average random samples.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Insurance companies can predict their total claims accurately even though individual claims are unpredictable, because the average over millions of policies is very close to its expectation. That same law makes a casino's small edge into a near-certain profit.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">X\\ge0</span> with <span class=\"m\">E[X] = 5</span>. What is the Markov bound on <span class=\"m\">P(X\\ge20)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/4|0.25\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">5/20</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">By Chebyshev, at most what fraction of any distribution lies 4 or more standard deviations from the mean?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/16|0.0625\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">1/k^2</span> with <span class=\"m\">k = 4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">i.i.d. values with variance 16 are averaged in groups of 4. What is the variance of the average?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2/n = 16/4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A fair coin shows heads 8 times in a row. The probability of tails on the next flip is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">more than 1/2, since tails is due</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">exactly 1/2</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">less than 1/2, since heads is hot</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Independent flips have no memory; the gambler's fallacy.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What does the law of large numbers say?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">The sample average converges to the expected value as the sample size grows</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">The number of heads minus tails converges to 0</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Every outcome eventually evens out exactly</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It's about averages; the count difference typically grows like <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt n</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">By Chebyshev, at least what fraction of values lie within 3 standard deviations of the mean?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"8/9|0.889\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">1 - 1/9</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "limit-theorems": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"a-surprising-shape\" data-h=\"3\">A surprising shape</h3>\n<p>Roll one die and the outcome is uniform: each face has probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac16</span>, a flat bar chart. Roll two dice and add them, and the totals form a triangle peaking at 7. Roll five dice and the total's distribution already looks like a smooth hump. Roll thirty and it's almost a perfect bell curve. Nothing about a die is bell-shaped, yet the bell appears. This happens with almost any starting distribution: skewed waiting times, lopsided coin flips, weird bimodal distributions. Add up enough independent copies and the total is approximately normal.</p>\n<p>This is the <strong>central limit theorem</strong> (CLT), and it explains why the normal distribution is everywhere. Heights, measurement errors and test scores are each the combined effect of many small independent influences, so they come out roughly normal. It is also the reason statistics works: it tells us the distribution of a sample average even when we know almost nothing about the population.</p>\n<h3 id=\"from-the-law-of-large-numbers-to-the-clt\" data-h=\"3\">From the law of large numbers to the CLT</h3>\n<p>The law of large numbers, from the previous lesson, says the sample mean <span class=\"m\">\\bar X_n</span> of <span class=\"m\">n</span> i.i.d. values converges to the population mean <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span>. Its spread shrinks: its standard deviation is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}</span>. But the law says nothing about the <em>shape</em> of the distribution of <span class=\"m\">\\bar X_n</span> for large but finite <span class=\"m\">n</span>. The CLT fills in that gap. It says that if we zoom in on <span class=\"m\">\\bar X_n</span> at exactly the right scale, by standardizing, we see a standard normal.</p>\n<h3 id=\"statement-of-the-theorem\" data-h=\"3\">Statement of the theorem</h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"m\">X_1, \\dots, X_n</span> be i.i.d. with mean <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span> and finite variance <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2 &gt; 0</span>. Then for large <span class=\"m\">n</span>,\n<span class=\"mb\">\\bar X_n\\approx N\\Big(\\mu, \\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\Big),\\qquad\\text{equivalently}\\qquad Z_n = \\frac{\\bar X_n - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n}\\approx N(0, 1).</span>\nFor the sum <span class=\"m\">S_n = X_1 + \\dots + X_n</span>, the same statement reads <span class=\"m\">S_n\\approx N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2)</span>. Precisely, <span class=\"m\">P(Z_n\\le z)\\to\\Phi(z)</span> for every <span class=\"m\">z</span> as <span class=\"m\">n\\to\\infty</span>.</p>\n<p>Three things are notable. First, the mean and variance aren't new: we already knew <span class=\"m\">E[\\bar X_n] = \\mu</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X_n) = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}</span> from linearity and independence. The new information is the normal shape. Second, the starting distribution can be almost anything, as long as it has a finite variance. Third, the result is an approximation, and how large <span class=\"m\">n</span> must be depends on the starting distribution. For symmetric distributions, <span class=\"m\">n = 10</span> is often plenty. For strongly skewed ones like the exponential, you may need 30 or more, and for very skewed ones like rare-event indicators, many more. The rule of thumb \"<span class=\"m\">n\\ge30</span>\" is a rough guide, not a law.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Central limit theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For i.i.d. values with mean <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span> and finite variance <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2</span>, the standardized sample mean <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\bar X_n - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n}</span> has approximately a standard normal distribution when <span class=\"m\">n</span> is large.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-standard-error\" data-h=\"3\">The standard error</h3>\n<p>The standard deviation of the sample mean, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}</span>, is so important that it has its own name: the <strong>standard error</strong> (SE). It tells you how far a sample average typically lands from the true mean. Because of the square root, quadrupling the sample size only halves the standard error. A survey of 100 people with <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 20</span> has SE 2; to get SE 1 you need 400 people, and to get SE 0.5 you need 1600.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Standard error</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The standard deviation of an estimator; for the sample mean it is <span class=\"m\">\\sigma/\\sqrt n</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"using-the-clt-a-worked-example\" data-h=\"3\">Using the CLT: a worked example</h3>\n<p>A coffee shop's daily customer counts are i.i.d. with mean 200 and standard deviation 40, and the distribution is somewhat skewed. What's the probability that over a 25-day month the average is above 210?</p>\n<p>The CLT says <span class=\"m\">\\bar X_{25}\\approx N(200, \\frac{40^2}{25})</span>, so the standard error is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{40}{5} = 8</span>. The z-score of 210 is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{210 - 200}{8} = 1.25</span>, and <span class=\"m\">P(Z &gt; 1.25) = 1 - 0.8944 = 0.1056</span>. About a 10.6% chance. We did this without knowing the daily distribution's shape.</p>\n<p>The same question about the monthly <em>total</em> is equivalent: the total is <span class=\"m\">25\\bar X</span>, approximately <span class=\"m\">N(5000, 25\\times1600) = N(5000, 200^2)</span>, and a total above 5250 has z-score <span class=\"m\">\\frac{250}{200} = 1.25</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-normal-approximation-to-the-binomial-explained\" data-h=\"3\">The normal approximation to the binomial, explained</h3>\n<p>A binomial count is a sum of <span class=\"m\">n</span> independent Bernoulli indicators, each with mean <span class=\"m\">p</span> and variance <span class=\"m\">p(1-p)</span>. By the CLT, <span class=\"m\">\\text{Bin}(n, p)\\approx N(np, np(1-p))</span> for large <span class=\"m\">n</span>. This is where the normal approximation in the normal-distribution lesson comes from. The sample proportion <span class=\"m\">\\hat p = X/n</span> is approximately <span class=\"m\">N\\big(p, \\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\big)</span>, which is the basis of every poll's margin of error. For <span class=\"m\">p = 0.5</span> and <span class=\"m\">n = 1000</span>, the standard error is <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{0.25/1000}\\approx0.0158</span>, and 95% of polls land within <span class=\"m\">1.96\\times0.0158\\approx0.031</span> of the truth: the familiar \"plus or minus 3 points.\"</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works-intuitively\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works, intuitively</h3>\n<p>Every time you add an independent variable, you smooth the distribution of the total: adding two dice blurs the flat shape into a triangle, and further additions keep blurring. Information about the starting shape, such as skewness, gets diluted; skewness of the sum shrinks like <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{\\sqrt n}</span>. The only features that survive are the mean and variance, and the normal is the one distribution determined by just those two. A rigorous proof uses moment generating functions or characteristic functions: the logarithm of the characteristic function of <span class=\"m\">Z_n</span> converges to <span class=\"m\">-\\frac{t^2}{2}</span>, which is that of <span class=\"m\">N(0,1)</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The CLT is about the distribution of the <strong>average or sum</strong>, not of individual values. Collecting more data does not make the data themselves normal; a histogram of 10,000 incomes is still skewed. It needs independence (or weak dependence) and finite variance: for heavy-tailed distributions like the Cauchy, averages never settle down. Don't confuse <span class=\"m\">\\sigma</span> (spread of individual values) with <span class=\"m\">\\sigma/\\sqrt n</span> (spread of the average). And remember it's an approximation that can be poor for small <span class=\"m\">n</span> or very skewed data.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The CLT says sums and averages of many i.i.d. values are approximately normal, with the average distributed as <span class=\"m\">N(\\mu, \\sigma^2/n)</span>. The standard error <span class=\"m\">\\sigma/\\sqrt n</span> measures the precision of a sample mean and shrinks like <span class=\"m\">1/\\sqrt n</span>. Standardize with <span class=\"m\">z = \\frac{\\bar x - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n}</span> to compute approximate probabilities. The CLT underlies polls, confidence intervals and hypothesis tests.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"clt\" data-args='{&quot;src&quot;: &quot;exponential&quot;, &quot;n&quot;: 2}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The top shows a skewed population. The bottom shows 4000 sample means of size n. Increase n.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Start with the exponential population and <span class=\"m\">n = 1</span>: the sample means look just like the population, skewed right.\n2. Move <span class=\"m\">n</span> to 2, 5, 10 and then 30. Watch the histogram of means become symmetric and bell-shaped, and narrower.\n3. Switch the population to \"coin (0/1)\". At <span class=\"m\">n = 1</span> there are only two bars; by <span class=\"m\">n = 30</span> there is a bell.\n4. Try \"bimodal\": a population with a hole in the middle still produces bell-shaped averages.\n5. Compare widths at <span class=\"m\">n = 4</span> and <span class=\"m\">n = 16</span>. The second should be about half as wide.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the centre of the sample means stays at <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span> while the width shrinks like <span class=\"m\">\\sigma/\\sqrt n</span>, the standard error. The shape becomes normal regardless of the population, faster for symmetric populations and slower for skewed ones like the exponential.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\bar X_n\\approx N\\Big(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\Big),\\qquad S_n\\approx N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">Z = \\frac{\\bar X_n - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n}\\approx N(0,1)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{SE}(\\bar X) = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\hat p\\approx N\\Big(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\Big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Bin}(n,p)\\approx N(np, np(1-p))</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Central limit theorem (Lindeberg–Lévy)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">X_1, X_2, \\dots</span> are i.i.d. with mean <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span> and variance <span class=\"m\">0 &lt; \\sigma^2 &lt; \\infty</span>, then for every real <span class=\"m\">z</span>, <span class=\"m\">P\\Big(\\frac{\\bar X_n - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n}\\le z\\Big)\\to\\Phi(z)</span> as <span class=\"m\">n\\to\\infty</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Sketch. Let <span class=\"m\">Y_i = (X_i - \\mu)/\\sigma</span>, so <span class=\"m\">Z_n = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt n}\\sum Y_i</span>. With characteristic function <span class=\"m\">\\varphi(t) = E[e^{itY}] = 1 - \\frac{t^2}{2} + o(t^2)</span> (since <span class=\"m\">E[Y] = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">E[Y^2] = 1</span>), independence gives <span class=\"m\">E[e^{itZ_n}] = \\varphi(t/\\sqrt n)^n = \\big(1 - \\frac{t^2}{2n} + o(\\frac1n)\\big)^n\\to e^{-t^2/2}</span>, the characteristic function of <span class=\"m\">N(0,1)</span>. Lévy's continuity theorem then gives convergence of the CDFs.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A die is rolled 100 times. Approximate P(total ≥ 370).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>One die: <span class=\"m\">\\mu = 3.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2 = \\frac{35}{12}</span>. Total: mean 350, variance <span class=\"m\">\\frac{3500}{12}\\approx291.7</span>, SD <span class=\"m\">\\approx17.08</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">z = \\frac{370 - 350}{17.08}\\approx1.17</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(Z\\ge1.17)\\approx1 - 0.879 = 0.121</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Light bulbs have lifetimes with mean 800 hours and SD 100. Approximate P(average of 64 bulbs &lt; 775 hours).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\text{SE} = \\frac{100}{\\sqrt{64}} = 12.5</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">z = \\frac{775 - 800}{12.5} = -2</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(Z &lt; -2)\\approx0.0228</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A poll of n = 400 voters is taken when the true support is p = 0.5. Approximate P(sample proportion &gt; 0.55).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\text{SE} = \\sqrt{\\frac{0.25}{400}} = 0.025</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">z = \\frac{0.55 - 0.5}{0.025} = 2</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(Z &gt; 2)\\approx0.0228</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An elevator has a maximum load of 1600 kg. Adult weights have mean 75 kg and standard deviation 15 kg. If 20 adults get in, approximately what is the probability the load limit is exceeded? What assumption does your answer depend on?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>The total <span class=\"m\">S\\approx N(20\\times75, 20\\times15^2) = N(1500, 4500)</span>, SD <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{4500}\\approx67.1</span>. <span class=\"m\">z = \\frac{1600 - 1500}{67.1}\\approx1.49</span>, so <span class=\"m\">P(S &gt; 1600)\\approx1 - \\Phi(1.49) = 1 - 0.9319\\approx0.068</span>. It assumes the weights are independent (a group of rugby players would violate this) and that <span class=\"m\">n = 20</span> is large enough for the normal approximation.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The CLT is why mini-batch gradients are roughly Gaussian around the true gradient, why we report model accuracy with error bars of <span class=\"m\">\\pm1.96\\sqrt{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)/n}</span>, and why many learning algorithms assume Gaussian noise. Ensembles and bootstrap estimates rely on averages behaving normally.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Load balancers and capacity planners model total traffic from many independent users as approximately normal, which lets them size servers for, say, the 99.9th percentile. Benchmarking reports average run times with standard errors because individual run times are noisy.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Pollsters report a margin of error of about <span class=\"m\">\\pm3</span> points for 1000 respondents because the CLT makes the sample proportion approximately normal with standard error near 0.016. Quality control charts plot averages of small batches, which the CLT makes approximately normal even if single measurements aren't.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Population SD is 12 and <span class=\"m\">n = 36</span>. What is the standard error of the mean?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">12/\\sqrt{36} = 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">To halve the standard error, multiply the sample size by...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>SE scales like <span class=\"m\">1/\\sqrt n</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Does the CLT say that a large sample of incomes will itself look normal?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No, it says the sample average is approximately normal</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The data keep their shape; only averages and sums become normal.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">i.i.d. values have mean 10 and SD 4. For <span class=\"m\">n = 64</span>, the sample mean is approximately normal with what mean?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"10\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The center doesn't change; only the spread shrinks.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Same setup: what is <span class=\"m\">P(\\bar X &gt; 11)</span> approximately? (Use <span class=\"m\">\\Phi(2) = 0.9772</span>.)</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.0228\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>SE <span class=\"m\">= 4/8 = 0.5</span>, so <span class=\"m\">z = 2</span> and <span class=\"m\">1 - 0.9772</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which population makes the sample mean approach normality fastest?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">A symmetric uniform distribution</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">A highly skewed distribution</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">They all converge equally fast</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Skewness slows convergence; symmetric populations need small <span class=\"m\">n</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "markov-chains": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"randomness-that-unfolds-over-time\" data-h=\"3\">Randomness that unfolds over time</h3>\n<p>So far our random variables have mostly been single draws or collections of independent ones. But many random things evolve step by step, and each step depends on where we are now. Tomorrow's weather depends on today's. The next word in a sentence depends on the current one. A visitor's next page on a website depends on the page they're reading. A board game piece moves from square to square according to dice rolls. A <strong>Markov chain</strong> is the simplest model of such a process, and it is remarkably powerful: it powers Google's original PageRank, text generators, queueing models and the sampling algorithms at the heart of Bayesian machine learning.</p>\n<h3 id=\"states-and-the-markov-property\" data-h=\"3\">States and the Markov property</h3>\n<p>A Markov chain moves among a set of <strong>states</strong>. Let's use weather with three states: Sunny, Cloudy and Rainy. Each day the chain is in one state, and we write <span class=\"m\">X_t</span> for the state on day <span class=\"m\">t</span>. The defining assumption is the <strong>Markov property</strong>: the probability of tomorrow's state depends only on today's state, not on the earlier history.\n<span class=\"mb\">P(X_{t+1} = j\\mid X_t = i, X_{t-1}, \\dots, X_0) = P(X_{t+1} = j\\mid X_t = i).</span>\nIn words, \"the future depends on the past only through the present.\" This doesn't mean yesterday doesn't matter at all; it means everything relevant about the past is captured by where you are now.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Markov chain</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A sequence of random states <span class=\"m\">X_0, X_1, \\dots</span> in which the distribution of <span class=\"m\">X_{t+1}</span> depends only on <span class=\"m\">X_t</span> (the Markov property).</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-transition-matrix\" data-h=\"3\">The transition matrix</h3>\n<p>Because only the current state matters, the whole chain is described by the <strong>transition probabilities</strong> <span class=\"m\">P_{ij} = P(X_{t+1} = j\\mid X_t = i)</span>, arranged in a <strong>transition matrix</strong>. For our weather:\n<span class=\"mb\">P = \\begin{pmatrix}0.7 &amp; 0.2 &amp; 0.1\\\\ 0.3 &amp; 0.4 &amp; 0.3\\\\ 0.2 &amp; 0.4 &amp; 0.4\\end{pmatrix}</span>\nwith rows and columns in the order Sunny, Cloudy, Rainy. Row <span class=\"m\">i</span> lists where you go from state <span class=\"m\">i</span>: from Sunny, it stays sunny with probability 0.7, turns cloudy with 0.2 and rainy with 0.1. Every entry is non-negative and <strong>every row sums to 1</strong>, because from any state you must go somewhere. We can draw the chain as a diagram with a circle for each state and an arrow from <span class=\"m\">i</span> to <span class=\"m\">j</span> labelled <span class=\"m\">P_{ij}</span>, including self-loops for staying put.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Transition matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The matrix <span class=\"m\">P</span> with entries <span class=\"m\">P_{ij} = P(X_{t+1} = j\\mid X_t = i)</span>. Its entries are non-negative and each row sums to 1.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"multi-step-probabilities\" data-h=\"3\">Multi-step probabilities</h3>\n<p>If today is Sunny, what's the chance it's Sunny two days from now? Add up the ways to get there: Sunny→Sunny→Sunny has probability <span class=\"m\">0.7\\times0.7 = 0.49</span>; Sunny→Cloudy→Sunny has <span class=\"m\">0.2\\times0.3 = 0.06</span>; Sunny→Rainy→Sunny has <span class=\"m\">0.1\\times0.2 = 0.02</span>. The total is <span class=\"m\">0.57</span>. That sum over intermediate states is exactly a row-times-column product from matrix multiplication, so the two-step transition probabilities are the entries of <span class=\"m\">P^2</span>, and in general the <span class=\"m\">n</span>-step probabilities are the entries of <span class=\"m\">P^n</span>.</p>\n<p>It's often clearer to track the whole <strong>distribution</strong> over states as a row vector. If <span class=\"m\">\\pi_t</span> gives the probabilities of each state on day <span class=\"m\">t</span>, then\n<span class=\"mb\">\\pi_{t+1} = \\pi_t P,\\qquad \\pi_t = \\pi_0P^t.</span>\nStarting from a known Sunny day, <span class=\"m\">\\pi_0 = (1, 0, 0)</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">\\pi_1 = (0.7, 0.2, 0.1)</span>, the first row of <span class=\"m\">P</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">\\pi_2 = (0.57, 0.26, 0.17)</span>, matching our path calculation for Sunny. Each entry is checked the same way, for example Rainy: <span class=\"m\">0.7(0.1) + 0.2(0.3) + 0.1(0.4) = 0.17</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-stationary-distribution\" data-h=\"3\">The stationary distribution</h3>\n<p>Keep multiplying and something striking happens: the distribution stops changing. Whether you start on a sunny or a rainy day, after a couple of weeks <span class=\"m\">\\pi_t</span> is essentially <span class=\"m\">(0.462, 0.308, 0.231)</span>. That limit is the <strong>stationary distribution</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\pi</span>, the distribution that one more step leaves unchanged:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\pi = \\pi P,\\qquad \\sum_i\\pi_i = 1.</span>\nFor the weather chain we can solve these linear equations exactly and get <span class=\"m\">\\pi = \\big(\\frac{6}{13}, \\frac{4}{13}, \\frac{3}{13}\\big)</span>. Check the Sunny entry: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{6(0.7) + 4(0.3) + 3(0.2)}{13} = \\frac{4.2 + 1.2 + 0.6}{13} = \\frac{6}{13}</span>. In linear algebra terms, <span class=\"m\">\\pi</span> is a left eigenvector of <span class=\"m\">P</span> with eigenvalue 1.</p>\n<p>The stationary distribution has two meanings. It's the long-run probability of each state, regardless of the starting state. And it's the long-run <strong>fraction of time</strong> the chain spends in each state: over many years, about <span class=\"m\">\\frac{6}{13}\\approx46\\%</span> of days are sunny. That second fact is a law of large numbers for Markov chains, called the ergodic theorem.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Stationary distribution</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A probability vector <span class=\"m\">\\pi</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\pi = \\pi P</span>: if the chain starts with distribution <span class=\"m\">\\pi</span>, it keeps that distribution forever.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"a-two-state-chain-you-can-solve-by-hand\" data-h=\"3\">A two-state chain you can solve by hand</h3>\n<p>Take a machine that is either Working (A) or Broken (B). Each day a working machine breaks with probability <span class=\"m\">p</span>, and a broken one is repaired with probability <span class=\"m\">q</span>. In the long run, the flow from A to B must balance the flow from B to A: <span class=\"m\">\\pi_A p = \\pi_B q</span>. Together with <span class=\"m\">\\pi_A + \\pi_B = 1</span>, this gives\n<span class=\"mb\">\\pi_A = \\frac{q}{p + q},\\qquad\\pi_B = \\frac{p}{p + q}.</span>\nWith <span class=\"m\">p = 0.3</span> and <span class=\"m\">q = 0.1</span>, the machine is working only a quarter of the time. This \"flow in equals flow out\" balance is a quick way to find stationary distributions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"when-does-the-chain-settle-down\" data-h=\"3\">When does the chain settle down?</h3>\n<p>Convergence to a unique stationary distribution needs two conditions. The chain must be <strong>irreducible</strong>: every state can be reached from every other, so the chain doesn't split into separate pieces. And it must be <strong>aperiodic</strong>: it doesn't cycle with a fixed rhythm. A chain that deterministically alternates A, B, A, B has stationary distribution <span class=\"m\">(\\frac12, \\frac12)</span>, but <span class=\"m\">\\pi_t</span> flips back and forth forever without converging. Self-loops, like the 0.7 chance of Sunny staying Sunny, destroy periodicity. A chain that is irreducible and aperiodic with finitely many states always converges to its unique stationary distribution from any start.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Don't mix up rows and columns: in our convention row <span class=\"m\">i</span> holds the probabilities of leaving <span class=\"m\">i</span>, and rows sum to 1. Multiply the distribution as a <em>row</em> vector on the left, <span class=\"m\">\\pi P</span>. Don't assume a real process is Markov without thinking: if weather really depends on the past three days, you can still use a Markov chain by making each state a triple of days. And remember the stationary distribution describes the long run, not tomorrow; tomorrow depends heavily on today.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A Markov chain moves between states with probabilities that depend only on the current state, collected in a transition matrix whose rows sum to 1. Distributions evolve by <span class=\"m\">\\pi_{t+1} = \\pi_tP</span>, so <span class=\"m\">n</span>-step probabilities come from <span class=\"m\">P^n</span>. An irreducible, aperiodic chain converges to a unique stationary distribution solving <span class=\"m\">\\pi = \\pi P</span>, which also gives the long-run fraction of time in each state.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g06-markov\" data-args='{&quot;chain&quot;: &quot;weather&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The weather chain from the article. Walk it, and compare the visit fractions with the stationary distribution.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Choose the weather chain, start in Sunny, and look at the probability-evolution plot: the curves start at (1, 0, 0) and flatten out at the dashed lines within about 10 steps.\n2. Change the start state to Rainy. The early curves differ, but they end at the same dashed lines, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{6}{13}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{4}{13}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{3}{13}</span>.\n3. Press \"Walk 1 step\" a few times to watch the walker move, then \"Walk 500 steps\" several times and compare the visit fractions with the stationary values.\n4. Switch to the two-state chain with <span class=\"m\">P(A\\to B) = 0.3</span> and <span class=\"m\">P(B\\to A) = 0.1</span>; check that <span class=\"m\">\\pi_A = 0.25</span>. Then set both sliders to 1 and see the periodic chain flip back and forth without settling.\n5. Try the web surfer chain and find which page gets the most long-run traffic.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the long-run answer forgets the starting state, which is the convergence theorem from the article. The walker's visit fractions approach the same numbers as <span class=\"m\">\\pi_t</span>, so \"long-run probability\" and \"fraction of time\" agree. Removing self-loops and making the chain alternate breaks convergence, which is the aperiodicity condition.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">P(X_{t+1}=j\\mid X_t=i, \\text{past}) = P_{ij},\\qquad \\sum_j P_{ij} = 1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\pi_{t+1} = \\pi_tP,\\qquad \\pi_t = \\pi_0P^t</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(X_{t+n} = j\\mid X_t = i) = (P^n)_{ij}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\pi = \\pi P,\\qquad \\sum_i\\pi_i = 1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{two-state: } \\pi = \\Big(\\frac{q}{p+q}, \\frac{p}{p+q}\\Big)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Chapman–Kolmogorov equations</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a Markov chain, <span class=\"m\">(P^{m+n})_{ij} = \\sum_k(P^m)_{ik}(P^n)_{kj}</span>: the probability of going from <span class=\"m\">i</span> to <span class=\"m\">j</span> in <span class=\"m\">m+n</span> steps sums over the state <span class=\"m\">k</span> reached after <span class=\"m\">m</span> steps.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>By the law of total probability over <span class=\"m\">X_{t+m} = k</span>: <span class=\"m\">P(X_{t+m+n} = j\\mid X_t = i) = \\sum_k P(X_{t+m} = k\\mid X_t = i)\\,P(X_{t+m+n} = j\\mid X_{t+m} = k, X_t = i)</span>. By the Markov property the last factor is <span class=\"m\">(P^n)_{kj}</span>, and the first is <span class=\"m\">(P^m)_{ik}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Convergence to the stationary distribution</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If a finite Markov chain is irreducible and aperiodic, it has a unique stationary distribution <span class=\"m\">\\pi</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\pi_0P^t\\to\\pi</span> for every starting distribution <span class=\"m\">\\pi_0</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">For the weather chain, it's Cloudy today. Find P(Rainy in 2 days).</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>One step from Cloudy: <span class=\"m\">(0.3, 0.4, 0.3)</span>.</li>\n<li>Rainy next: <span class=\"m\">0.3(0.1) + 0.4(0.3) + 0.3(0.4) = 0.03 + 0.12 + 0.12 = 0.27</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A customer buys brand X or Y each week. X-buyers switch to Y with probability 0.2; Y-buyers switch to X with probability 0.4. Find the long-run market share of X.</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Two-state chain with <span class=\"m\">p = 0.2</span> (X→Y) and <span class=\"m\">q = 0.4</span> (Y→X).</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\pi_X = \\frac{q}{p+q} = \\frac{0.4}{0.6} = \\frac23</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Verify that π = (6/13, 4/13, 3/13) satisfies the Cloudy equation of π = πP.</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Cloudy column of <span class=\"m\">P</span>: <span class=\"m\">(0.2, 0.4, 0.4)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\frac{6(0.2) + 4(0.4) + 3(0.4)}{13} = \\frac{1.2 + 1.6 + 1.2}{13} = \\frac{4}{13}</span>. It matches.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A frog jumps between lily pads 1 and 2. From pad 1 it jumps to pad 2 with probability 0.5 and otherwise stays; from pad 2 it jumps to pad 1 with probability 0.25 and otherwise stays. (a) Write the transition matrix. (b) If it starts on pad 1, find the distribution after 2 jumps. (c) Find the stationary distribution.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">P = \\begin{pmatrix}0.5 &amp; 0.5\\\\0.25 &amp; 0.75\\end{pmatrix}</span>. (b) <span class=\"m\">\\pi_1 = (0.5, 0.5)</span>; <span class=\"m\">\\pi_2 = (0.5\\cdot0.5 + 0.5\\cdot0.25,\\ 0.5\\cdot0.5 + 0.5\\cdot0.75) = (0.375, 0.625)</span>. (c) <span class=\"m\">\\pi_1(0.5) = \\pi_2(0.25)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\pi_1 + \\pi_2 = 1</span> give <span class=\"m\">\\pi = (\\frac13, \\frac23)</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) methods such as Metropolis–Hastings and Gibbs sampling build a chain whose stationary distribution is a Bayesian posterior that is too hard to compute directly, then run it and use the visited states as samples. Hidden Markov models power speech recognition and sequence labelling, and reinforcement learning is formulated as a Markov decision process. Simple n-gram language models are Markov chains over words.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Google's PageRank models a random surfer who follows links (and occasionally jumps to a random page). A page's rank is its probability in the stationary distribution, computed by repeatedly multiplying by the transition matrix (power iteration). Queueing systems, network protocols and cache models are analysed as Markov chains.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Board games like Snakes and Ladders are Markov chains; you can compute the expected number of turns to finish. Credit-rating agencies use transition matrices for the probability a bond moves from rating A to B in a year, and epidemiologists model patients moving between healthy, sick and recovered states.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In a transition matrix, what must each row sum to?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>From any state the chain must go somewhere.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Two-state chain with <span class=\"m\">p = 0.2</span> (A→B) and <span class=\"m\">q = 0.3</span> (B→A). Find <span class=\"m\">\\pi_A</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3/5|0.6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">q/(p+q) = 0.3/0.5</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Weather chain, Sunny today. What is P(Sunny tomorrow)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.7|7/10\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The Sunny-to-Sunny entry of <span class=\"m\">P</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Weather chain, Sunny today. What is P(Sunny in 2 days)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.57\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">0.49 + 0.06 + 0.02</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The Markov property says the next state depends on...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">only the current state</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the entire history of states</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">nothing; states are independent</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The present carries all the relevant information about the past.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A chain alternates deterministically A, B, A, B, ... Does <span class=\"m\">\\pi_t</span> converge?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes, to (1/2, 1/2)</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No, it is periodic</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The stationary distribution is (1/2, 1/2), but the chain is periodic and <span class=\"m\">\\pi_t</span> keeps flipping.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>"}, "formulas": [{"l": "axioms", "t": "Sample spaces, events and the axioms of probability", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">P(A)\\ge0,\\qquad P(S) = 1,\\qquad P(A\\cup B) = P(A)+P(B)\\ \\text{ if } A\\cap B=\\emptyset</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A^c) = 1 - P(A)\\qquad P(\\emptyset) = 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A\\cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A\\cap B)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A) = \\frac{|A|}{|S|}\\quad\\text{(equally likely outcomes)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A\\cup B\\cup C) = P(A)+P(B)+P(C) - P(A\\cap B) - P(A\\cap C) - P(B\\cap C) + P(A\\cap B\\cap C)</span></p>"}, {"l": "counting-probability", "t": "Counting outcomes: permutations and combinations", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{ordered, with repeats: } n^k</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">n! = n(n-1)\\cdots 1,\\quad 0! = 1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(n,k) = \\frac{n!}{(n-k)!}\\qquad \\binom nk = \\frac{n!}{k!\\,(n-k)!}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\binom nk = \\binom n{n-k}\\qquad \\binom nk = \\binom{n-1}{k-1} + \\binom{n-1}{k}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{multiset arrangements: } \\frac{n!}{n_1!\\,n_2!\\cdots n_r!}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(a+b)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^n\\binom nk a^kb^{n-k}</span></p>"}, {"l": "conditional", "t": "Conditional probability and independence", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">P(A\\mid B) = \\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A\\cap B) = P(A\\mid B)P(B) = P(B\\mid A)P(A)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A\\cap B\\cap C) = P(A)P(B\\mid A)P(C\\mid A\\cap B)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A) = \\sum_i P(A\\mid B_i)P(B_i)\\quad (B_i \\text{ a partition})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{independent: } P(A\\cap B) = P(A)P(B)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(A^c\\mid B) = 1 - P(A\\mid B)</span></p>"}, {"l": "bayes", "t": "Bayes' theorem", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">P(H\\mid E) = \\frac{P(E\\mid H)P(H)}{P(E)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(E) = P(E\\mid H)P(H) + P(E\\mid H^c)P(H^c)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(H_i\\mid E) = \\frac{P(E\\mid H_i)P(H_i)}{\\sum_j P(E\\mid H_j)P(H_j)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{posterior odds} = \\frac{P(E\\mid H)}{P(E\\mid H^c)}\\times\\text{prior odds}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">p(\\theta\\mid\\text{data})\\propto p(\\text{data}\\mid\\theta)\\,p(\\theta)</span></p>"}, {"l": "discrete-rv", "t": "Discrete random variables", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">p(x) = P(X = x),\\quad p(x)\\ge0,\\quad \\sum_x p(x) = 1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">F(x) = P(X\\le x) = \\sum_{t\\le x}p(t)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">E[X] = \\sum_x x\\,p(x),\\qquad E[g(X)] = \\sum_x g(x)p(x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{Var}(X) = E[(X-\\mu)^2] = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sigma_X = \\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var}(X)}</span></p>"}, {"l": "expectation-properties", "t": "Rules for expectation and variance", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">E[aX + b] = aE[X] + b,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(aX + b) = a^2\\operatorname{Var}(X)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">E\\Big[\\sum_i a_iX_i\\Big] = \\sum_i a_iE[X_i]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">E[I_A] = P(A)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y) = E[XY] - E[X]E[Y]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{Var}(X \\pm Y) = \\operatorname{Var}(X) + \\operatorname{Var}(Y) \\pm 2\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">X_i \\text{ i.i.d.}: \\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X) = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}</span></p>"}, {"l": "discrete-dists", "t": "Common discrete distributions", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{Bernoulli}(p): \\; E = p,\\; \\operatorname{Var} = p(1-p)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Bin}(n,p): \\; P(k) = \\binom nk p^k(1-p)^{n-k},\\; E = np,\\; \\operatorname{Var} = np(1-p)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Geom}(p): \\; P(k) = (1-p)^{k-1}p,\\; E = \\tfrac1p,\\; \\operatorname{Var} = \\tfrac{1-p}{p^2}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Poisson}(\\lambda): \\; P(k) = \\frac{\\lambda^ke^{-\\lambda}}{k!},\\; E = \\operatorname{Var} = \\lambda</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Uniform}\\{a..b\\}: \\; E = \\tfrac{a+b}2,\\; \\operatorname{Var} = \\tfrac{m^2-1}{12},\\; m = b-a+1</span></p>"}, {"l": "continuous-rv", "t": "Continuous random variables", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">P(a\\le X\\le b) = \\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx = F(b) - F(a)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f(x)\\ge0,\\quad \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x)\\,dx = 1,\\quad f(x) = F'(x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">E[X] = \\int xf(x)\\,dx,\\quad \\operatorname{Var}(X) = \\int x^2f(x)\\,dx - \\mu^2</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Uniform}[a,b]: \\; f = \\tfrac{1}{b-a},\\; E = \\tfrac{a+b}{2},\\; \\operatorname{Var} = \\tfrac{(b-a)^2}{12}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Exp}(\\lambda): \\; f = \\lambda e^{-\\lambda t},\\; F = 1 - e^{-\\lambda t},\\; E = \\tfrac1\\lambda,\\; \\operatorname{Var} = \\tfrac{1}{\\lambda^2}</span></p>"}, {"l": "normal-distribution", "t": "The normal distribution", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">f(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-\\frac{(x-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">Z = \\frac{X - \\mu}{\\sigma}\\sim N(0,1),\\qquad P(X\\le x) = \\Phi\\!\\Big(\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\Big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\Phi(-z) = 1 - \\Phi(z)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(|Z|\\le1)\\approx0.683,\\ P(|Z|\\le2)\\approx0.954,\\ P(|Z|\\le3)\\approx0.997</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">aX + b\\sim N(a\\mu + b, a^2\\sigma^2)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">X + Y\\sim N(\\mu_1+\\mu_2, \\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2)\\ \\text{(independent)}</span></p>"}, {"l": "joint", "t": "Joint distributions, covariance and correlation", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">p_X(x) = \\sum_y p(x,y),\\qquad f_X(x) = \\int f(x,y)\\,dy</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">p(y\\mid x) = \\frac{p(x,y)}{p_X(x)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{independent}\\iff p(x,y) = p_X(x)p_Y(y)\\ \\text{for all } x,y</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y) = E[XY] - E[X]E[Y]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\rho = \\frac{\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)}{\\sigma_X\\sigma_Y},\\qquad -1\\le\\rho\\le1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{Cov}(aX+b, cY+d) = ac\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)</span></p>"}, {"l": "inequalities-lln", "t": "Probability inequalities and the law of large numbers", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">P(X\\ge a)\\le\\frac{E[X]}{a}\\quad(X\\ge0)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(|X-\\mu|\\ge k\\sigma)\\le\\frac{1}{k^2}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">E[\\bar X_n] = \\mu,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X_n) = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(|\\bar X_n - \\mu|\\ge\\varepsilon)\\le\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n\\varepsilon^2}\\to0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Monte Carlo: } \\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n g(X_i)\\to E[g(X)]</span></p>"}, {"l": "limit-theorems", "t": "The central limit theorem", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\bar X_n\\approx N\\Big(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\Big),\\qquad S_n\\approx N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">Z = \\frac{\\bar X_n - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n}\\approx N(0,1)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{SE}(\\bar X) = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\hat p\\approx N\\Big(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\Big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Bin}(n,p)\\approx N(np, np(1-p))</span></p>"}, {"l": "markov-chains", "t": "Markov chains", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">P(X_{t+1}=j\\mid X_t=i, \\text{past}) = P_{ij},\\qquad \\sum_j P_{ij} = 1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\pi_{t+1} = \\pi_tP,\\qquad \\pi_t = \\pi_0P^t</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P(X_{t+n} = j\\mid X_t = i) = (P^n)_{ij}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\pi = \\pi P,\\qquad \\sum_i\\pi_i = 1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{two-state: } \\pi = \\Big(\\frac{q}{p+q}, \\frac{p}{p+q}\\Big)</span></p>"}]});
